2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Castile, Duchess of York
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Castile, Duchess of York (c. 1355 – 23 December 1392) was a daughter of King Peter of Castile and María de Padilla who was of Jewish descent. [1] She was a younger sister of Constance of Castille.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1372, sometime between the 1st of March and 30th of April, Isabella married Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York, fourth son of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault at Wallingford. As a result of her marriage, she beca
2 CONC me the first of a total of eleven women who became (as a courtesy by marriage to their husbands) Duchess of York. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward of Norwich, 2nd Duke of York (1373 - 25 October 1415).
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of York (1374 - 29 November 1416). Married Thomas le Despenser and was mother of Isabel le Despenser, Countess of Worcester and Warwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard of Conisburgh, 3rd Earl of Cambridge (1375 - 5 August 1415).
2 CONT
2 CONT She was named a Lady Companion of the Order of the Garter in 1378. On 14 January 1393 she was buried in Kings Langley Manor House in Hertfordshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Isabella of Castile, Duchess of York (c. 1355 – 23 December 1392) was a daughter of King Peter of Castile and María de Padilla.[1] She was a younger sister of Constance, Duchess of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1372, sometime between the 1 March and 30 April, Isabella married Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York, fourth son of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault at Wallingford. As a result of her marriage, she became the fir
2 CONC st of a total of eleven women who became (as a courtesy by marriage to their husbands) Duchess of York. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward of Norwich, 2nd Duke of York (1373 – 25 October 1415).
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of York (1374 – 29 November 1416). Married Thomas le Despenser and was mother of Isabel le Despenser, Countess of Worcester and Warwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard of Conisburgh, 3rd Earl of Cambridge (1375 – 5 August 1415).
2 CONT
2 CONT She was named a Lady Companion of the Order of the Garter in 1378. Isabella died 23 December 1392 and on 14 January 1393 was buried in Kings Langley Manor House in Hertfordshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infanta_Isabella_of_Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_of_the_Garter
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Isabella of Castile, Duchess of York was the daughter of King Peter of Castile and María de Padilla. She was a younger sister of Constance, Duchess of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 1, 1372, Isabella married Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York, he was the fourth son of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault, at Wallingford, England. As a result of her marriage, she became the first of a total
2 CONC  of eleven women who became Duchess of York. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward of Norwich, 2nd Duke of York (1373 – 25 October 1415).
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of York (1374 – 29 November 1416). Married Thomas le Despenser and was mother of Isabel le Despenser, Countess of Worcester and Warwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard of Conisburgh, 3rd Earl of Cambridge (1375 – 5 August 1415).
2 CONT
2 CONT She was named a Lady Companion of the Order of the Garter in 1378. Isabella died 23 December 1392 and on 14 January 1393 was buried in Kings Langley Manor House in Hertfordshire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel lies entombed with her husband in King's Langley.  By the terms of her will, dated December 6, 1392, she asked that a hundred trentals and a hundred sauters were to be said for her soul, and four priests, or one at least, w
2 CONC ere to sing for her by the space of four years.  Upon the day of her burial her best horse was to be delivered for her mortuary.  She bequeathed to the King her heart of pearls; to the Duke of Lancaster, a tablet of jasper, given 
2 CONC her by the King of Armenia; to her son Edward, her crown, to remain to his heirs; to Constance le Despencer, her daughter, a fret of pearls; to the Duchess of Gloucester, her tablet of gold with images, and also her sauter with th
2 CONC e arms of Northampton; and to the King the residue of her goods, in trust that he should allow his godson Richard, her younger son, an annuity of 500 marks for life, a trust which the King, out of the great respect he bore to her,
2 CONC  accepted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally interred in the Church of the Friary at Langley, the remains of the Duke and his wife were brought to All Saint's, King's Langley, about the year 1574.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple were destined for a second exhumation. On November 22, 1877, Professor George Rolleston, M.D. The professor was expecting to find two remains instead he found three. The remains are those of Isabella of Castile, her hus
2 CONC band Edmund of Langley and the third are those of their daughter in law Anne Mortimer
2 CONT
2 CONT Family links: 
2 CONT
