3 CONT
3 CONT The burial site of Richard III either York or Leicester.  Even though York the York Minster holds services and they are sometimes regarded as on the High Church or Anglo-Catholic end of the Anglican continuum. It is still not a Ca
3 CONC tholic church, nor is the Leicester Cathedral.  That said either way he would not entombed in a Catholic Church.  So here is the important point. He was a King. Now what matters no matter what you conjure up, Kings for the most pa
3 CONC rt want to be recognized. In his case he definitely wanted to be KING. 
3 CONT Lets look at a little history: Richard was a royal prince until the death of his brother Edward IV in 1483. Appointed as protector of his nephew, Edward V, Richard instead assumed the reins of power.
3 CONT Edward and his brother Richard, known as the Princes in the Tower, disappeared soon after. Rumors circulated they had been murdered on the orders of their uncle.
3 CONT
3 CONT Where would the most amounts of people pay tribute to see his tomb?
3 CONT
3 CONT York is located 211 miles from London, three and half to four hours away on a good day.
3 CONT Long trip unless you stay over. Less people likely to visit. Less recognition. 
3 CONT
3 CONT Leicester is located 100 miles north of London, two, two and half-hours away. 
3 CONT A very nice days outing. 
3 CONT REMEMDER: Richard III wanted to be King and this King wanted recognition. 
3 CONT He did not die in battle while hiding. He wanted to lead and be seen. He was out there on the battlefield. Bury his remains in Leicester where he will be visited the most, get the recognition he craved for and this the site where 
3 CONC the Battle of Bosworth took place in 1485 and infamous as the place where King Richard III lost his life and crown to Henry Tudor and thus where the Tudor dynasty was born. Richard III King of England, died defending this ground. 
3 CONC Bury him there. 
3 CONT
3 CONT Walter G. Ashworth 16th Great Nephew of Richard III King of England
3 CONT Great Grandson of:
3 CONT • Uther Pendragon "The Head Dragon" ap Custennyn, King of Britons • c. 430 – 476
3 CONT •  William the Conqueror, King of the English • 1024 - 1087
3 CONT •  Henry I Beauclerc, King of the English • 1068 - 1135
3 CONT •  Henry II, King of The English  • 1133 – 1189
3 CONT    John I “Lackland” Plantagenet, King of England • 1166 - 1216
3 CONT    Henry III King of England  • 1207-1272
3 CONT    Edward I “Longshanks” King of England • 1239-1307
3 CONT    Edward II King of England • 1284  - 1327
3 CONT    Edward III King of England • 1312 -1377
3 CONT    Philippa de Hainault (Counts of Holland), Queen consort of England, 
3 CONT    married to Edward III, King of England • 1312 – 1369
3 CONT     John of Gaunt 1st Duke of Lancaster • 1340 – 1399
3 CONT Great Nephew of:
3 CONT Arthwyr Pendragon ap Uthyr, (King Arthur) High King of Britain • 460 - 518 
3 CONT Henry "The Young King", King of England • 1155 - 1183 
3 CONT Richard the Lionheart, King of England • 1157 – 1199 
3 CONT Edward “The Black Prince” 1st Knight, Order of the Garter • 1330 -1376 
3 CONT Henry IV, King of England • 1367 - 1413 
2 NOTE Thing to always remember about King Richard III
3 CONT The negative perception of Richard III relates to some or all of the following points:
3 CONT he was a nasty hunchback who plotted and schemed his way to the throne;
3 CONT he killed Henry VI's son Edward;
3 CONT he killed Henry VI (a sweet, innocent saint);
3 CONT he got his brother, the duke of Clarence, executed;
3 CONT he killed the Princes in the Tower (sweet, innocent children);
3 CONT he killed his wife Anne because he wanted to marry his niece Elizabeth;
3 CONT he was a bad king;
3 CONT and so it was lucky that Good King Henry Tudor got rid of him for us.
2 NOTE Richard III of England is my 16th great uncle.
2 NOTE http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-leicestershire-21245346
2 NOTE 'Humped backs'
3 CONT The reconstruction is particularly important because there are no surviving contemporary portraits of Richard III.
3 CONT
3 CONT Dr Ashdown-Hill said: "All the surviving portraits of him - even the very later ones with humped backs and things which were obviously later additions - facially are quite similar [to each other] so it has always been assumed that
3 CONC  they were based on a contemporary portrait painted in his lifetime or possibly several portraits painted in his lifetime."
3 CONT
3 CONT The Richard III Society officially unveiled the reconstruction on Tuesday morning, at the Society of Antiquaries in London.
3 CONT
3 CONT It is expected the reconstruction will be put on public display in future.
3 CONT
3 CONT Experts from the University of Leicester said DNA from the skeleton matches that of a descendant of Richard III's family.
3 CONT
3 CONT Dr Ashdown-Hill said: "We weren't certain whether the body was Richard III so the facial reconstruction - particularly if it hadn't been possible to get DNA from the bones - might have been an additional piece of evidence, and sti
3 CONC ll is."
3 CONT
3 CONT Caroline Wilkinson, professor of craniofacial identification at the University of Dundee, said: "It was a great privilege to be able to analyse a skull thought to be Richard III.
3 CONT
3 CONT "The facial reconstruction was produced on the assumption that the remains were unknown and portraits of Richard III were not used as reference.
3 CONT
3 CONT "When the 3D digital bust was complete it was replicated in plastic using a rapid prototyping system and this was painted, prosthetic eyes added and dressed with a wig, hat and clothing."
3 CONT
3 CONT Prof Wilkinson said the Dundee team artist, Janice Aitken, only used the portraits of Richard III at this stage as reference for hair style and colour, eye colour, skin colour and clothing.
3 CONT
3 CONT The team estimated the end result was as accurate as possible.
3 CONT
3 CONT "Our facial reconstruction methods have been blind tested many times using living subjects and we know that we can expect that approximately 70% of the facial surface should have less than 2mm of error," said Prof Wilkinson.
