2 TITL Compared to Cartoon Version
2 NOTE A facial reconstruction based on the skull of Richard III has revealed how the English king may have looked.
3 CONT
3 CONT The king's skeleton was found under a car park in Leicester during an archaeological dig.
3 CONT
3 CONT The reconstructed face has a slightly arched nose and prominent chin, similar to features shown in portraits of Richard III painted after his death.
3 CONT
3 CONT Historian and author John Ashdown-Hill said seeing it was "almost like being face to face with a real person".
3 CONT
3 CONT The development comes after archaeologists from the University of Leicester confirmed the skeleton found last year was the 15th Century king's, with DNA from the bones having matched that of descendants of the monarch's family.
3 CONT
3 CONT 'Very handsome'
3 CONT Richard was killed in the Battle of Bosworth in 1485, at the age of 32 and after just two years on the throne, having been challenged by the forces of Henry Tudor, the future Henry VII.
3 CONT
3 CONT Continue reading the main story
3 CONT
3 CONT Interactive: Twisted bones reveal a king
3 CONT Dr Ashdown-Hill, who wrote The Last Days of Richard III, said: "The most obvious features in portraits are the shape of the nose and the chin and both of those are visible in the facial reconstruction."
3 CONT
3 CONT Richard III Society member Philippa Langley, originator of the search, said on a Channel 4 documentary earlier: "It doesn't look like the face of a tyrant. I'm sorry but it doesn't.
3 CONT
3 CONT "He's very handsome. It's like you could just talk to him, have a conversation with him right now."
3 CONT
3 CONT Layers of muscle and skin were added by computer to a scan of the skull and the result was made into a three-dimensional plastic model.
3 CONT
3 CONT Dr Ashdown-Hill said: "I had said previously that when I stood by the grave in Leicester that I felt closer to Richard III than I had ever been, but when I saw the facial reconstruction I realised I had been close to a dead Richar
3 CONC d III.
3 CONT
3 CONT "It was just bones, just a body, whereas confronting a facial reconstruction, I felt almost in the presence of a living Richard III."
2 NOTE Richard III: Facial reconstruction shows king's features
3 CONT http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-leicestershire-21328380
