1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''William Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier'''1 
2 CONT M, #107437, '''d. before 26 June 1480'''
2 CONT Last Edited=16 Apr 2008
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=3.0%
2 CONT '''William Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier''' was the son of Henry Bourchier, 1st Earl of Essex and Isabella Plantagenet.1 '''He married, firstly, Isobel de Vere''', daughter of John de Vere, 12th Earl of Oxford.2 '''He married, sec
2 CONC ondly, Lady Anne Woodville''', daughter of Richard Wydevill, 1st Earl Rivers and Jacquetta de Luxembourg, '''before 15 August 1467.1,3 He died before 26 June 1480.'''2
2 CONT '''He was styled as Viscount Bourchier.1 He fought in the Battle of Barnet on 14 April 1471, fighting on the Yorkist side.'''3
2 CONT '''Children of William Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier and Lady Anne Woodville'''
2 CONT 1. Henry Bourchier, 2nd Earl of Essex+3 d. 13 Mar 1539/40
2 CONT 2. Cicely Bourchier+4
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1. [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 111. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families.
2 CONT 2. [S2] Peter W. Hammond, editor, The Complete Peerage or a  History of the House of Lords and All its Members From the Earliest Times, Volume XIV: Addenda & Corrigenda (Stroud, Gloucestershire, U.K.: Sutton Publishing, 1998), pag
2 CONC e 104. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage, Volume XIV.
2 CONT 3. [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extin
2 CONC ct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 249. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT 4. [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume II, page 250.
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p10744.htm#i107437
2 CONT _____
2 CONT '''William BOURCHIER (V. Bourchier)'''
2 CONT '''Father''': Henry BOURCHIER (1º E. Essex)
2 CONT '''Mother''': Isabel PLANTAGENET (C. Essex)
2 CONT '''Married: Anne WOODVILLE (V. Bourchier/C. Kent)'''
2 CONT '''Children''':
2 CONT 1. Henry BOURCHIER (2º E. Essex)
2 CONT 2. Isabel BOURCHIER
2 CONT 3. Cecille BOURCHIER (B. Ferrers of Chartley)
2 CONT From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/BOURCHIER1.htm#William%20BOURCHIER%20(V.%20Bourchier)
2 CONT ________
2 CONT '''Anne Woodville, Viscountess Bourchier''' (c. 1438 – 30 July 1489) was an English noblewoman. She was a younger sister of Queen Consort Elizabeth Woodville to whom she served as a lady-in-waiting. '''Anne was married twice; firs
2 CONC t to William Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier''', and secondly to George Grey, 2nd Earl of Kent. '''Anne was the grandmother of the disinherited adulteress''' Anne Bourchier, 7th Baroness Bourchier, '''and an ancestress of''' Robert 
2 CONC Devereux, 2nd Earl of Essex.
2 CONT Family
2 CONT '''Anne Woodville''' was born in about 1439 at Grafton Regis, Northamptonshire, the second eldest daughter, and one of the fourteen children of Richard Woodville, 1st Earl Rivers and Jacquetta of Luxembourg. Her elder sister was E
2 CONC lizabeth Woodville who would become Queen Consort of King Edward IV of England.
2 CONT Anne's paternal grandparents were Sir Richard Wydeville and Joan Bedlisgate, and her maternal grandparents were Peter I of Luxembourg, Count of Saint-Pol, Conversano and Brienne, and Margaret de Baux.
2 CONT In 1466, two years after her sister Elizabeth's secret marriage to King Edward, and one year after her coronation, Anne became one of Queen Elizabeth's ladies-in-waiting, receiving forty pounds a year for her services.[1]
2 CONT Marriages and issue
2 CONT '''Sometime before 15 August 1467, Anne married William Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier''', the son and heir of Henry Bourchier, 1st Earl of Essex, and Isabel of York.
2 CONT '''Anne's was just one of the many advantageous marriages''' Queen Elizabeth shrewdly arranged for her numerous siblings with eligible scions of the most aristocratic families in the realm; a scheme which was done with the purpose
2 CONC  of augmenting her family's power, prestige, and wealth. This blatantly ambitious, self-seeking policy of the Queen consort deeply antagonised the old nobility and House of Commons against the entire Woodville family.[2]
2 CONT One of the most formidable enemies of the Woodvilles was Richard Neville, 16th Earl of Warwick, a former Yorkist supporter who switched his allegiance to the Lancastrians following King Edward's marriage to Elizabeth. In 1469, War
2 CONC wick ordered the execution of '''Anne's father''', Earl Rivers and her brother, John. They had both been taken prisoner when the King's army was defeated by Warwick's forces at the Battle of Edgecote Moor.
2 CONT '''William and Anne received lands worth one hundred pounds a year'''.[1] Anne was briefly the owner of the manors of Nether Hall and Over Hall in the county of Suffolk. These had previously belonged to James Butler, 5th Earl of O
2 CONC rmond, a staunch supporter and favourite of Queen Margaret of Anjou. He was beheaded in 1461 following the crushing Lancastrian defeat at the Battle of Towton, and his properties were subsequently forfeited to the victorious Yorki
2 CONC st king, Edward IV.
2 CONT '''On 14 April 1471, William fought at the Battle of Barnet on the side of the Yorkists who won a decisive victory.
2 CONT Anne had three children by her first husband William''':
2 CONT *Henry Bourchier, 2nd Earl of Essex, 6th Baron Bourchier, Viscount Bourchier, 2nd Count of Eu (died 13 March 1540), married Mary Say, by whom he had one daughter, Anne Bourchier, suo jure 7th Baroness Bourchier who was the sole he
2 CONC iress to his titles and estates. She was the first wife of William Parr, 1st Marquess of Northampton whom she deserted to elope with her lover, thus creating a scandal which resulted in the forfeiture of her estates and most of he
2 CONC r titles.
2 CONT *Cecily Bourchier (died 1493), married John Devereux, 9th Baron Ferrers of Chartley, by whom she had a son, Walter Devereux, 1st Viscount Hereford, and a daughter, Anne Devereux. Robert Devereux, Earl of Essex (1565–1601) was a no
2 CONC table descendant of Walter who in his turn had married Mary Grey, the daughter of Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset and Cecily Bonville.
2 CONT *Isabel Bourchier (1477 – after 1500), died unmarried.
2 CONT '''William died on 26 June 1480. Shortly afterwards, Anne married''' George Grey, son and heir of Edmund Grey, 1st Earl of Kent. As George did not succeed to the title of Earl of Kent until 1490, Anne was never styled Countess of 
2 CONC Kent, due to her death in 1489.
2 CONT The marriage produced one son:
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_Woodville
2 CONT _______
2 CONT Sir John de Vere, 12th Earl Oxford1,2,3,4,5,6,7
2 CONT Last Edited	5 Mar 2014
2 CONT M, #22083, b. 23 April 1408, d. 26 February 1462
2 CONT Father	Sir Richard de Vere, 11th Earl Oxford2,8,9 b. 1385, d. 15 Feb 1417
2 CONT Mother	Alice Sergeaux2,8,9 b. c 1386, d. 18 May 1452
2 CONT Sir John de Vere, 12th Earl Oxford was born on 23 April 1408 at Hedingham Castle, Essex, England; Age 9 in 1417, age 44 in 1452.2,4,7 He married Elizabeth Howard, daughter of Sir John Howard, 7th Lord Plaiz and Joan Walton, betwee
2 CONC n 22 May 1425 and 31 August 1425; They had 5 sons (Sir Aubrey; Sir John, 13th Earl of Oxford; Sir Richard; Sir George; & Sir Thomas) and 3 daughters (Mary, a nun at Barking; Jane/Joan, wife of Sir William Norreys; & '''Elizabeth, 
2 CONC wife of William Bourchier''').10,2,3,4,5,6,7 Sir John de Vere, 12th Earl Oxford died on 26 February 1462 at Tower Hill, London, Middlesex, England, at age 53; Beheaded. Buried in the churchof Austin Friars, London.2,4,7
2 CONT Family	Elizabeth Howard b. c 1410, d. a 27 Jan 1476
2 CONT Children	
