1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===John Bourchier, 6th Baron Ferrers of Groby
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Bourchier,_6th_Baron_Ferrers_of_Groby
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Bourchier (c. 1438 – 1495) was a 15th-century English knight and nobleman. He was steward of the Honour of Richmond.[1] Bourchier fought in the Battle of Bosworth in 1485 on the side of King Richard III.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bourchier was the fourth son of Henry Bourchier, 1st Earl of Essex and his wife Isabel of Cambridge, Countess of Essex.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Before 2 May 1462, Bourchier married to the heiress Lady Elizabeth Grey, widow of Sir Edward Grey, jure uxoris 6th Baron Ferrers of Groby (d. 18 December 1457).[1] Lady Elizabeth was the granddaughter and heiress of William Ferrer
2 CONC s, 5th Baron Ferrers of Groby in her own right.[1] When Lady Ferrers remarried to Bourchier, he received the title of Baron Ferrers of Groby (jure uxoris).[1] They had no issue.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Lady Ferrers, Bourchier remarried to Elizabeth Chichele before 6 July 1490.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bourchier left a will dated 4 June 1495 in which he requested his burial in Bilegh Abbey, Essex, next to the tomb of his parents.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to some sources Bourchier died in Spain.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
2 CONT *  Douglas Richardson. Plantagenet Ancestry: A Study In Colonial And Medieval Families, 2nd Edition, 2011. pg 161–164.
2 CONT * https://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1919.htm#i57650
2 CONT *” Historical Personography. The Map of Early Modern London. Ed. Janelle Jenstad. Victoria: University of Victoria. Accessed September 15, 2020. https://mapoflondon.uvic.ca/historical_personography.htm Sir John Bourchier (fl. betw
2 CONC een 1404 and 1406) Husband of Elizabeth Ashton. Uncle of Henry Bourchier. Not to be confused with John Bourchier. 
2 CONT * [http://specialcollections.le.ac.uk/digital/api/collection/p15407coll6/id/5700/download “GENEALOGY of the LORDS of the MANOR of GROBY”]
