2 ADDR
3 CITY Caceres
3 STAE Extremadura
3 CTRY Spain
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1550
2 PLAC Puerto Principe, Cuba
2 ADDR
1 OCCU led conquest of Tampico, Conquistador, Explorer, see "About" section
1 TITL Capitán y Conquistador de Florida
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Regarding allegation that Vasco Porcallo was never in Mexico:
2 CONT
2 CONT Vasco Porcallo was the conqueror of Tampico, a vassal state of the Aztecs.  Hernan Cortes had requested his services during the conquest.  However, Porcallo's conduct regarding the natives was so egregious that charges were brough
2 CONC t against him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  I've added a source from PARES, a document from Hernan Cortes asking the Spanish court to essentially pardon Porcallo.  (http://pares.mcu.es/ParesBusquedas/servlets/Control_servlet?accion=3&txt_id_desc_ud=370940&fromagenda=N)
2 CONT
2 CONT The document was not catalogued in the archives until 1551.  The text reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sobrecédula al presidente y oidores de la Audiencia de México, sobre otra anterior que ordena que no se remuevan ni quiten a Vasco Porcallo, mientras que esté ausente, ciertos pueblos de indios que tiene en esa tierra, y que le f
2 CONC ueron dados por Don Hernando Cortés, en renumeración de sus servicios."
2 CONT
2 CONT (Entry submitted by Rosa Martha Villarreal)
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Encomendero; regidor y alcalde de PPE en diversos años.
2 CONT
2 CONT Los veteranos en 14 de junio de 1911 solicitaron que a la calle de Santa Rosa se le nominara Vasco Porcallo, lo cual fue aprobado por el Ayuntamiento de PPE en la Sesión
2 CONT
2 CONT del 20 de junio de 1911.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1544 visitó Puerto Príncipe el obispo fray Diego de Sarmiento Sotomayor, religioso cartujo que llegó con la expedición de Hernando de Soto.
2 CONT
2 CONT "En su informe, dado en La Habana el 25 de julio de 1544
2 CONT
2 CONT al Reino manifiesta que:
2 CONT
2 CONT En 30 de marzo vine a esta villa del Puerto del Príncipe,
2 CONT
2 CONT que visité. Hay 14 vecinos. En la comarca vive Vasco
2 CONT
2 CONT Porcallo de Figueroa, la más calificada persona de esta
2 CONT
2 CONT isla, de linaje y hacienda. Tiene en esta villa casadas 2
2 CONT
2 CONT hijas mestizas con dos de los principales de ella, y desde
2 CONT
2 CONT su cabaña,que por mar y por tierra dista de aquí 100
2 CONT
2 CONT leguas, viene siempre a residir aquí parte del año. Es
2 CONT
2 CONT generoso y animoso, y es mucha parte para sustentar esta villa y la de Sancti Spíritus. Todos le respetan como a
2 CONT
2 CONT padre, por sus buenas obras." Cultura y Costumbre 2005:27
2 CONT
2 CONT Tuvo otras dos  hijas doña Teresa de la Cerda y Sotomayor, casada con el capitán Esteban de Lagos Mexías, doña Leonor de la Cerda y Sotomayor, también esposa de un hombre principal en Puerto Príncipe, citadas por el obispo De Sarm
2 CONC iento como mestizas
2 CONT
2 CONT Cultura y Costmbre 2005.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ==Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa (1) was born in about 1484, or in 1494, in Cáceres, Extremadura, Spain. In 1522,==
2 CONT
2 CONT He stated at a trial that he was 28 years old, which, if true, would mean that he was born in 1494. He sailed to Española with Nicolás de Ovando, the newly appointed governor general of the Indies, arriving there on April 15, 1502
2 CONC . More than one source includes the report that he, Porcallo, fled Spain to escape the law, having murdered a cousin over a family inheritance. Historia de Familias Cubanas also states that, before he sailed to Española, he servid
2 CONC o [served] in Spain and Italy. From all these statements is appears more than likely that the 1484 date is closer tothe truth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovando's mission in Española was to subdue the indigenous Taino Indians, which he achieved within five years by either killing or enslaving practically the entire Indian population. Porcallo enthusiastically assisted Ovando in thi
2 CONC s work and for this he was given considerable land and quantities of Indians. In 1511, with Diego de Velázquez de Cuéllar, Porcallo passed to the conquest of the island of Cuba, where he founded the towns of Puerto Príncipe, Sanct
2 CONC i Spiritus, San Juan de los Remedios, and Trinidad. He owned a country property called La Sabana, located in either Trinidad or near Remedios, where he made a great fortune from his plantations and mines worked by his siboneye sla
2 CONC ves.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obispo [bishop] Diego Sarmiento wrote in 1544: In this district [Puerte Príncipe], Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa, the most qualified person in this island, lives off linaje [lineage] and country property. He has in this village two m
2 CONC estiza [mixed blood] daughters who are married to two of the most important men of the town and from his home [in Remedios], a hundred leagues distant, always comes to spend part of the year here. He is generous and brave and es m
2 CONC uchaparte [does much ???] to sustain this village and that of SanctiSpiritus. All respect him like a father for his good works.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was, however, and with good reason, hated and feared by the siboneyes. He traveled the countryside, raiding their towns and making slaves of those he caught. He, thus, became the most powerfuland rich encomendero [???], capturi
2 CONC ng thousands of siboneyes, selling the youths, feeding the old and weak to his dogs, and sending the young Indians to work on his country properties.
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to the cruelty of Porcallo and others like him, most of the Indian population was exterminated, many siboneyes preferring to commit suicide than to work for their Spanish masters. In 1522, Porcallo's treatment of his Indians w
2 CONC as questioned by a court alarmed at the reports of murders, mutilations, and other outrages, which had come to their ears.
2 CONT
2 CONT Porcallo denied none of the charges and added that he had once killed twelve Indians, cutting off their testicles, which he then forced them to eat, before burning them "as an example to the others". With few Indians left to work 
2 CONC the fields, the Spaniards were forced to look for African slaves, and in 1530, Vasco Porcallo brought to his country property the first black slaves, eight men, twelve women, and twelve children.
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived extravagantly in Trinidad and aided various expeditions leaving Cuba for new discoveries and conquests. He gave Pánfilo de Narváez "certain stores" before Narvaez set out on his ill fated voyage to Florida in 1527, and, o
2 CONC n June 7, 1538, when Hernando de Soto arrived as the new Governor of Cuba with a commission to sail to and claim Florida for the Spanish crown, Porcallo again offered his support, giving Soto provisions and Andalusian horses bred 
2 CONC on his property La Sabana. Soto was so well pleased that he offered Porcallo the position of second in command of the expedition, to which Porcallo agreed, and he caused the vessels to be well provisioned, giving a great many hogs
2 CONC  and loads of caçabe bread.
2 CONT
2 CONT With two of his sons, and with the rank of teniente general de la Armada [deputy general of the Armada], Porcallo accompanied adelantado Soto, arriving at the bay of Espiritu Santo (now Tampa Bay) on May 31,1539. It was a magnific
2 CONC ent expedition, but Porcallo remained inFlorida for only a short time - returning to Cuba due to his age. Other writers see the reason for his return a little differently.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fidalgo de Elvas, an unknown Portuguese, wrote that, as the principal object of Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa in coming to Florida had been to get slaves for his plantation and mines, finding, after some incursions, that no seizures 
2 CONC could be made, because of dense forest and extensive bogs, he determined to go back to Cuba; and in consequence of that resolution, there grow up such a difference between him and Soto, that neither of them treated nor spoke to th
2 CONC e other kindly. Still, with words of courtesy, he asked permission of him to return, and took his leave.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodrigo Ranjel wrote that Vasco Porcallo, upon his return from a raid, had something of a clash with the governor and it was accepted as a good settlement that Vasco Porcallo should return to Cuba to look after the affairs of the 
2 CONC government there, and to provide the governor and his army, when it should be necessary, with what they might have need.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rafael Farto wrote that a few days after the expedition arrived in the bay of Espiritu Santo, they were attacked by a large group of Indians and Porcallo, ahead of his men, turned them back, becoming the hero of the day. Soon afte
2 CONC r, however, he collapsed in a fit of trembling and, although he was only forty five years of age, being obese and suffering badly from gout, he decided to return immediately to Cuba.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return, Porcallo passed most of the time at La Sabana until 1550, when he died, at fifty six years of age, in Puerto Príncipe, where he was buried beneath the main altar of the parish church.
2 CONT
2 CONT In some genealogical trees, he appears married to Elvira de Mendoza, Spanish, according to others, to a young Indian princess called Tinima, the daughter of cacique [chief] Camagüebax. His many children include Elvira, Leonor, Ter
2 CONC esa, Gomez Juan de Figueroa, María, Vasco, Cristóbal, and Lorenzo Gómez Juarez de Figueroa. He had many more children than these eight, and José Barreiro wrote that Porcallo took dozens of Indian wives and spawned a generation of 
2 CONC more than a hundred mestizos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa and Tinima, Elvira de Mendoza, and others:
2 CONT
2 CONT i Elvira Lasso de la Vega [7th great grandmother of Perucho]
2 CONT
2 CONT ii Leonor de la Cerda (2) was born in Puerto Príncipe. She married Luis Diaz de Lagones, and they had three children, Cristóbal Porcallo, Inés de Mendoza, and María Manuel. Leonor then married Gonzalo de Lagos y de la Jardina, alc
2 CONC alde ordinario [magistrate] de Bayamo. They had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT iii Teresa de la Cerda Sotomayor y Casenda was born in Puerto Príncipe and married Capitán Esteban de Lagos Mexias (2), in Puerto Príncipe. He was regidor [councilman] and alcalde ordinario [magistrate] de Bayamo, son of Garcia de
2 CONC  Lagos, also regidor and alcalde ordinario de Bayamo, and Francisca Pérez. They had eight children, Gonzalo de la Cerda, Lorenzo Lasso de la Vega, Ana de Lagos, García Mejía de la Cerda, María de Mendoza, Elvira de Lagos, Teresa d
2 CONC e Lagos, and Esteban de Mendoza also called Esteban de la Cerda. Teresa made her will in Bayamo, in 1627, in which she "declara querer ser enterrada en lasepultura de su padre" [stated her wish to be buried in the sepulcher of her
2 CONC  father].
2 CONT
2 CONT iv Gomez Juan de Figueroa
2 CONT
2 CONT v Maria Porcallo de Figueroa was born in Puerto Príncipe and married Capitán Juan de Argote, a native of Andalusia, in San Salvador de Bayamo. He was born in about 1472, and died after 1562. Some historians say that Juan was born 
2 CONC in Bayamo, the son of an Indian chief and that he was raised by Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa (1). María and Juan had a daughter, Juana Manuel de Figueroa y Argote.
2 CONT
2 CONT vi Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa (2) was born in Puerto Príncipe, and was regidor [councilman] and alcalde ordinario [magistrate] de Puerto Príncipe. In 1539, que era muy joven [though he was very young], he accompanied his father an
2 CONC d older brother, Lorenzo, on the expedition to Florida undertaken by Hernando de Soto. He returned to Cuba with his father before long.
2 CONT
2 CONT vii Cristobal Porcallo de Figueroa was capitán de infanteria en la Habana and en la villa de Puerto Príncipe.
2 CONT
2 CONT viii Lorenzo Gomez Juarez de Figueroa was capitán de infanteria and teniente de gobernador de los territories colonizados por su padre [captain of infantry and lieutenant governor of the territories colonized by his father]. Pasó 
2 CONC a La Florida con el Adelantado Hernandez de Soto y tambien estuvo en el Peru cuando la rebelion de Pizarro [He went to Florida with the Adelantado Hernandez de Soto and was also in Peru at the time of the rebellion of Pizarro]. He
2 CONC  is mentioned briefly in a narrative of de Soto's expedition based on the diary of Rodrigo Ranjel who writes: On the 17th of March [1540?] they left Capachequi and at nightfall came to White Spring. ... The next day they came at n
2 CONC ightfall to the river Toa where they made two bridges; and the horse belonging to Lorenzo Juarez, son of VascoPorcallo was drowned.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fueron hijos suyos, probablemente, [They were his children, probably] Juan and Francisca, and sin duda [without a doubt], Francisco and Alonso Xuárez de Figueroa.
2 CONT
2 CONT  ______________
2 CONT
2 CONT Conquistador de Florida Inca(Estados Unidos Norteamerica)
2 CONT
2 CONT Garcilaso Vega
2 CONT https://books.google.co.cr/books?id=2AhCAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA252&lpg=PA252&dq=juan+ponce+de+leon+robo+oro+florida&source=bl&ots=_Z9YmgEadg&sig=8n1glUbGeKz1hyIbYKr_p3xXaK8&hl=es&sa=X&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=juan%20ponce%20de%20leon%20robo
2 CONC %20oro%20florida&f=false
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 11086. Vasco (i) Porcallo de Figueroa, born in Cáceres, España.; died 1550 in Puerto Príncipe (Camagüey), Cuba. (Source: Santa Cruz, IV, p. 306.). He was the son of 22172. Gutierre Porcallo de Sotomayor and 22173. Aldonza Manuel d
2 CONC e Figueroa.
2 CONT
2 CONT More About Vasco (i) Porcallo de Figueroa: Fact 2: Conquistador y Poblador de la Isla de Cuba.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of the information on this branch comes from a Message board of Eveleo de las Mercedes Astray Caneda y del Junco at https://www.ancestry.com/boards/localities.caribbean.cuba.general/6110.1/mb.ashx
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa was one of the founders of many settlements in early Cuba; he founded Puerto Principe (now Camaguey), Sancti-Spiritus, (and Remedios?). He inter-married himself and some of his family with the indigenous
2 CONC  people of Cuba. He became the wealthiest Spaniard in Cuba of his time.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1518 Spain was going to send an expedition to explore Mexico. Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa was chosen to lead the expedition, but he turned it down, and it then was offered to Hernan Cortes Information from page 57 of https://cub
2 CONC angenclub.org/upload/files/Historia_de_Sancti-Spiritus_-_Vol_I-_Rafael_Perez_y_Luna.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Possible family: On page 80 of  https://cubangenclub.org/upload/files/Historia_de_Sancti-Spiritus_-_Vol_I-_Rafael_Perez_y_Luna.pdf there is a mention of an Alonso Juarez de Figueroa, who is petitioning for a grant of land ca
2 CONC lled Mabuya in order to raise pigs. This individual does not show in other genealogical trees of Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa, but he does share a last name with some of Vasco's offsprings, and he is living in Sancti-Spiritus, where
2 CONC  Vasco had lived. On the same page there is a mention of Francisco Juarez de Figueroa, who is one of Vasco's children as a (regidor) in the city government of Sancti-Spiritus
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 4, 1629, Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa, from Sancti-Spiritus, Cuba married Antonia Millan. This is not the Conquistador Vasco Porcallo de Figueroa that was born around 1490 and died in 1550. Not his son, Vasco Porcallo de 
2 CONC Figueroa who was born around 1530. This is probably one of his descendants down the line. Information from page 127 of https://cubangenclub.org/upload/files/Historia_de_Sancti-Spiritus_-_Vol_I-_Rafael_Perez_y_Luna.pdf 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1535, Manuel de Rojas y Cordoba, Governor of the Island of Fernandina (as Cuba was known by at the time), sent a letter to Spain informing the Crown that many of the Spanish Conquistadors had married indigenous wives, like: Vas
2 CONC co Porcallo de Figueroa (who was living in Sancti-Spiritus, at the time), Jorge Vazquez, Alonso de Cepeda, Juan Rodriguez de Cordoba, Sebastian de la Fuente, and Alonso de Oviedo. In 1529, Isabel Rodriguez, who had been married to
2 CONC  Fernando de Orozco, was given an indian called "Gasparico" who was her brother. In 1526, there is documentation that Juan Alonso, of Trinidad, was married to Catalina Mendez, who was (india de la tierra) indigenous. Information f
2 CONC rom page 11 of https://cubangenclub.org/upload/files/2010_S_F_Final_ENS.pdf
