1 OCCU 1st Baron Dudley, Lord Lieutenant of Ireland, Count de Clonard, Viceroy of Ireland, st Baron Dudley, Baron of Dudley
1 TITL Sir Lord Lieutenant of Ireland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Sutton,_1st_Baron_Dudley
2 CONT
2 CONT Photo: Dudley Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Middle Ages the Sutton family inherited the title and estate of Lord Dudley and lived in Dudley Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John de Sutton VI - was born on 25 Dec 1400 in Dudley Castle, Staffordshire, England and died on 30 Sep 1487 in Staffordshire, England and was buried in St James Priory, Dudley, Staffordshire, England . He was the son of Sir J
2 CONC ohn de Sutton V and Constance Blount. Sir John married Elizabeth Berkeley. Elizabeth was born before 1422 in Beverstone, Gloucestershire, England. She was the daughter of Sir John Berkeley and Elizabeth Betteshorne. She died about
2 CONC  8 Dec 1478 and was buried in St. James Priory, Dudley, Staffordshire, England .
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John - Only in 1432 with the succession of John de Sutton VI did the barony come to prominence for he had a long and successful career in the service of the royal court. Amongst his many appointments he held the lieutenancy of
2 CONC  Ireland. In the Wars of the Roses he supported the Lancasterian faction under Henry VI and was imprisoned at Ludlow Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1455 he was captured at the battle of St Albans and again imprisoned. Despite his previous loyalty to the Lancastrian cause he was soon employed on diplomatic missions for the Yorkists acting as ambassador in negotiations with 
2 CONC Burgundy and Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Sutton VI survived the slaughter of these wars and died in 1487 after a long and most distinguished career.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Lord Steward in 1422 he brought home the body of King Henry V to England, and was chief mourner and carried the standard of King Henry V at his funeral. he fought throughout the wars with France and was a firm supporter of the 
2 CONC House of Lancaster in the Wars of the Roses. He was summoned to Parliament from 15 Feb 1439/40 by writs directed Johanni de Sutton de Duddeley militi, whereby he is held to have become Lord Dudley. He was the first of his family t
2 CONC o adopt the surname of Dudley as an alias for Sutton. He was taken prisoner with King Henry VI on 23 May 1455 at the first Battle of St. Albans, and was wounded at Blore Heath on 23 Sep 1459. Children: (Quick Family Chart) i. 	Sir
2 CONC  Edmund Sutton was born after 1422, lived in Dudley,Worcestershire,England and died after 6 Jul 1483 . Sir Edmund married Joyce de Tiptoft about 1450. Joyce was born about 1430. She died in 1485 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Edmund - married Joyce de Tiptoft about 1430and later Matilda Clifford ii. 	Jane Sutton was born about 1434, lived in Dudley Castle, Staffordshire, England. Jane married Thomas Mainwaring. Thomas was born about 1450 in Ightfie
2 CONC ld , SW of Whitchurch, Shropshire, England. He was the son of William Mainwaring and Margaret Warren. He died in 1508 . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Name: JOHN VI Sutton  Given Name: JOHN VI  Surname: Sutton  Prefix: Sir  Suffix: (1st Lord Dudley)  Sex: M  Birth: 25 Dec 1400 in Dudley Castle, Staffordshire, England  Christening: Barton, Derbyshire, England  Death: 30 Sep 1487 
2 CONC in Staffordshire, England  Burial: St. James Priory, Dudley, Staffordshire, England  Change Date: 21 Sep 2005 at 15:22
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: JOHN De Sutton Dudley b: Feb 1379-1380 in Coleshill, Arden, Warwickshire, England c: in Dudley, Warwickshire, England  Mother: Constance-Constantine Blount b: 1380 in Barton, Blount, Burton-upon-Trent, Derbyshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 ELIZABETH de Berkeley b: Abt 1388 in Beverstone, Gloucestershire, England Married: Abt 1421 in England  Change Date: 21 Sep 2005 Children
2 CONT  Edmund Sutton b: Bet 1425 and 1427 in of Dudley Castle, Worcestershire, England
2 CONT  John Dudley b: Abt 1427 in Of Hatherington, Climping, Sussex, Eng
2 CONT  Jane Sutton Dudley b: Abt 1434 in of Dudley Castle, Staffordshire, England
2 CONT  Eleanor (Dudley) Sutton b: Abt 1440 in of Dudley, Staffordshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT  John Sutton, 1st Baron Dudley (December 25, 1400 – September 30, 1487), was Lord Lieutenant of Ireland 1428-30.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at Barton-under-Needwood, Staffordshire, became 1st Baron Dudley and a Knight of the Garter, and died at Stafford, Staffordshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Father was John Sutton Vth.
2 CONT
2 CONT John 1st Baron Dudley married Elizabeth de Berkeley of Beverstone, sometime after March 14, 1420. The sons of Dudley by this marriage were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund Sutton,  John Sutton Dudley Kt. of Atherington.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Dudley Bishop Of Durham: (1476-1483)  Oliver Dudley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  Thorns among the roses Holly Forrest Tamer Web: http://awt.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=tamer&id=I14436 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Viceroy of Ireland in 1429
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Only in 1432 with the succession of John de Sutton VI did the barony come to prominence for he had a long and successful career in the service of the royal court. Amongst his many appointments he held the lieutenancy of Ireland. I
2 CONC n the Wars of the Roses he supported the Lancasterian faction under Henry VI and was imprisoned at Ludlow Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1455 he was captured at the battle of St Albans and again imprisoned. Despite his previous loyalty to the Lancastrian cause he was soon employed on diplomatic missions for the Yorkists acting as ambassador in negotiations with 
2 CONC Burgundy and Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT John de Sutton VI survived the slaughter of these wars and died in 1487 after a long and most distinguished career.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Lord Steward in 1422 he brought home the body of King Henry V to England, and was chief mourner and carried the standard of King Henry V at his funeral. he fought throughout the wars with France and was a firm supporter of the 
2 CONC House of Lancaster in the Wars of the Roses. He was summoned to Parliament from 15 Feb 1439/40 by writs directed Johanni de Sutton de Duddeley militi, whereby he is held to have become Lord Dudley. He was the first of his family t
2 CONC o adopt the surname of Dudley as an alias for Sutton. He was taken prisoner with King Henry VI on 23 May 1455 at the first Battle of St. Albans, and was wounded at Blore Heath on 23 Sep 1459.
2 CONT
2 CONT [source: http://www.renderplus.com/hartgen/htm/de-sutton.htm#name1718]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Sutton,_1st_Baron_Dudley
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John Sutton, 1st Baron Dudley (December 25, 1400 – September 30, 1487), was Lord Lieutenant of Ireland 1428-30.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at Barton-under-Needwood, Staffordshire, became 1st Baron Dudley and a Knight of the Garter, and died at Stafford, Staffordshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Father was John Sutton Vth.
2 CONT
2 CONT John 1st Baron Dudley married Elizabeth de Berkeley of Beverstone, sometime after March 14, 1420. The sons of Dudley by this marriage were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Edmund Sutton,  John Sutton Dudley Kt. of Atherington.  William Dudley Bishop Of Durham: (1476-1483)  Oliver Dudley.  As Lord Steward in 1422 he brought home the body of King Henry V to England, and was chief mourner and standard 
2 CONC bearer at his funeral. He fought in several campaigns throughout the period of the wars with France and was a resolute defender of the House of Lancaster early on in the Wars of the Roses, but changed his allegiance for political 
2 CONC safety along with many others later on.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dudley was summoned to Parliament from February 15, 1440, by writs directed: 'Johanni de Sutton de Duddeley militi', whereby he obtained a Barony by writ as Lord Dudley. He was the first of his family to adopt the surname of Dudle
2 CONC y as an alias for Sutton. "John Dudley, Knyght, Lord Dudley" died testate in his 87th year. His will is dated August 17, 1487.
2 CONT
2 CONT The barony was inherited by his son, Edmund de Sutton, 2nd Baron Dudley, whose son was Edward 2nd lord Dudley.
2 CONT
2 CONT Battle of St Albans 1455
2 CONT
2 CONT Found amongst the Lancastrian army at the first Battle of St Albans on 22 May 1455 were John Sutton of Dudley, Worcestershire and his son Edmund Sutton of Dudley, Worcestershire. Lord Dudley was taken prisoner along with King Henr
2 CONC y VI.  Battle of Blore Heath 1459
2 CONT
2 CONT Dudley was present at the Battle of Blore Heath on September 23, 1459, along with his son Edmund Sutton. Dudley was wounded and captured.  Battle of Towton 1461
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Dudley was rewarded after the battle for his participation on the side of Edward, Earl of March, son of Richard, Duke of York. On June 28 of that year, Edward IV was proclaimed King in London.  Battle of Bosworth Field 1485
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the battle, Dudley was created Sheriff of Sussex by the new King Henry VII.  Political offices  Preceded by ' Lord Lieutenant of Ireland 1428–1430 Succeeded by '  Peerage of England  New title Baron Dudley 1440–1487 Succ
2 CONC eeded by Edward Sutton 
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Sutton,_1st_Baron_Dudley"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
