2 CONC U#v=onepage&q=Thomas%20LEIGH%201509&f=false A Genealogical History of the Dormant: Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire. By Sir Bernard Burke.]
2 CONT * [http://archive.org/stream/agenealogicalhi00burkgoog#page/n74/mode/1up '''A Genealogical History of the Dormant: Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire.''' By Sir Bernard Burke. Pg.54]
2 CONT * '''SIR JOHN LE BLOUNT''', then thirty-nine years of age, who was in the service of the Earl of Lancaster, and had obtained from that nobleman a grant for life of the manor of Passingham, co. Northampton. He had also lands from t
2 CONC he earl in Holland and Duffield, co Derby, and Tiberton, in Gloucestershire. he had two wives, 1st ISOLDA, dau. and heir of Sir Thomas de Mountjoy, by whom he acquired a large accession of estates, and had issue,
2 CONT ** JOHN (Sir), who m. twice, 1st, Juliana, dau. of __ Foulhurst, and 2ndly, Isabella, dau. and heir of Sir Bryan Cornwall, of Kinlet, co. Salop, whose heir-general is WILLIAM LACON CHILDE, Esq., of Kinlet. By his 1st wife, Juliana
2 CONC , Sir John had a son,
2 CONT *** JOHN BLOUNT, of Sodington, ancestor of the BLOUNTS, Baronets of Sodington.
2 CONT **  Walter, d.s.p.
2 CONT * Sir John Blount's 2nd wife was Eleanor, 2nd dau. of John Beauchamp, of Hache, co Somerset, and widow of John Meriet, of Meriet, in the same shire. By this lady he left at his decease, 32nd HENRY III. (1358), a son, the heroic
2 CONT *   SIR WALTER BLOUNT, so celebrated for his martial prowess in
2 CONT * http://archive.org/stream/agenealogicalhi00burkgoog#page/n75/mode/1up Pg. 55
2 CONT * the warlike times of EDWARD III., RICHARD II., and HENRY IV., and immortalized by the muse of Shakespeare for his devotion, even unto death, to King HENRY. Sir Walter fell at the battle of Shrewsbury, 22 June, 1403, wherein, bei
2 CONC ng standard bearer, he was arrayed in the same armour as his royal master, and was slain, according to the poet, in single combat, by the Earl of Douglas, who had supposed he was contending with the king himself. 
2 CONT *In 1367 we find Sir Walter accompanying the BLACK PRINCE and his brother, the DUKE OF LANCASTER (John of Gaunt), upon the expedition into Spain to aid PETER THE CRUEL, King of Castle, and assisting on 3 April in that year at the 
2 CONC battle of Najore, which restored PETER to his throne. Thenceforward for a series of years, indeed until the prince's decease, he appears to have been immediatley and confidentially attached to the duke, having chosen his wife, who
2 CONC m he m. about the year 1372, from amongst the ladies in the suite of CONSTANTIA OF CASTILE (eldest dau. of PETER, and his successor on the throne, who became the royal consort of JOHN OF GAUNT) when the princess visited England in
2 CONC  1369. In 1398 the duke granted 100 marks a-year to Sir Walter for the good services which had been rendered to him by the knight and his wife, the Lady Sancia. The Lady Sancia's maiden designation was DONNA SANCHA DE AYALA; she w
2 CONC as the dau. of DON DIEGO GOMEZ DE TOLEDO, alcalde mahor and chief justice of Toledo, and notario mayor or principal secretary of the kingdom of Castile, by is wife, Inez Alfon de Ayala, on of the most ancient and illustrious house
2 CONC s in Spain. JOHN OF GAUNT, at his decease, appointed Sir Walter one of his executors, and bequeathed him a legacy of 100 points, L6 6s. 8d.
2 CONT *   In 1374, Sir Walter's half-brother, Sir John Blount, of Sodington, conveyed to him numerous manors, which he had inherited from his mother, Isolda, heiress of the Mountjoy family. In 1381 he became proprietor, by purchase, of 
2 CONC the large estates of the BAKEPULS family, in cos. Derby, Stafford, Leicester, and Hertford. In 1385 he obtained a charter for a fair and free warren in his demesne lands at Barton, and other manors in Derbyshire. In 1399 he was ra
2 CONC nger of Needwood Forest and knight of the shire for co. Derby. By his wife, Donna Sancha, who survived him, and lived until 1418, he left issue, .... etc.
2 CONT * Sir Walter was s. by his eldest son,
2 CONT * SIR JOHN BLOUNT, K.G., who was governor of Calais, and defeated in Aquitaine, in 1412, a French army commanded by a marshal of France, for which achievement he was created a knight of the Garter the next year. In 1418, when Roue
2 CONC n was besieged by King HENRY V., Sir John Blount assisted at the siege. He d. without issue, and was s. by his brother,
2 CONT * SIR THOMAS BLOUNT, treasurer of Normandy, who was then seated at Elwaston, in Derbyshire, and to whom the Duke of Exeter gave 1,000 marks (L666 6s. 8.d.) to found a charity at Leicester. Sir Thomas m. 1st, Margaret, dau. of Sir 
2 CONC Thomas Greseley, Knt., of Greseley, and dying in 1456, left issue,
2 CONT ** I. WALTER (Sir), Lord High Treasurer of England, of Whose line we treat.
2 CONT ** '''II. THOMAS (Sir), of Milton Ross, co. Leicester, ancestor of the BLOUNTS of Maple Durham, co. Oxford, still resident at that seat. (See BURKE'S Landed Gentry.)
2 CONT ** I. Elizabeth, m. to Ralph Shirley, of West Heston, in Sussex.
2 CONT ** II. Sanchia, m. to Edward Langford.
2 CONT ** III. Agnes, m. to __ Wolseley.
2 CONT *The eldest son,
2 CONT * WALTER BLOUNT, was made treasurer of Calais, 39th of HENRY VI., and had the same office confirmed to him upon the accession of King EDWARD IV. In the 4th year of with latter monarch's reign, he was constituted, by letters patent
2 CONC , dated 24 November, LORD TREASURER OF ENGLAND, and the next year, advanced, by charter, dated 20 June, to the peerage, by the title of BARON MOUNTJOY, of Thurveston, co Derby. This nobleman became to stanch an adherent of the Hou
2 CONC se of York, that he shared largely in the confiscated estates of the leading Lancastrians--particularly in those of Sir William Carey, Sir William Vaux, and Thomas Courtenay, Earl of Devon. obtaining thereby extensive territorial 
2 CONC possessions in the counties of Devon, Cornwall, and Worcester. He was also honoured with the Garter. His lordship m. 1st, Helena, dau. of Sir John Byron, of Clayton, Lancashire; and by her (who was buried at Grey Friars, London) h
2 CONC e had three sons; .... etc. ________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Books
2 CONT * ''The Marbury Ancestry by Meredith Bright Colke''t (The Magee Press, Philadelphia, 1936)
2 CONT ** * Sir Thomas Blount. b. abt 1420 of Derbyshire & Melton Roos Abbey, Lincolnshire ENG. d. in 1468.
2 CONT ** abt 1453 Thomas married Agnes Hawley. ______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''From''' [http://www.riggs.org.uk/OldSite/IREBlount.htm Extracts from the Family Trees of Charles Blount, 8th Lord Montjoy - Lord Deputy / Lieutenant of Ireland 1600-1604, and Oliver St. John, 1st Viscount Grandison - Lord Lie
2 CONC utenant of Ireland 1615-1622]
2 CONT ** '''Parents:'''
2 CONT *** '''Father:''' Sir Thomas Blount (d 1456, Treasurer of Normandy) younger brother of Sir John Blount, Governor of Calais
2 CONT *** '''Mother:''' m1. Margaret Gresley (dau of Sir Thomas Gresley of Gresley)
2 CONT ** '''Sibling generation:'''
2 CONT ** 1.	Sir Walter BLOUNT, 1st Lord Mountjoy (b c1420, d 01.08.1474), m1. (by 1442) Ellen Byron (dau of Sir John Byron of Clayton)
2 CONT ** 2. Sir Thomas Blount of Milton (or Melton) Ross (d 1468), 
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Spouses of Thomas:'''
2 CONT ** m1. Anna Hally (dau of John Hally), 
2 CONT ** m2. Margaret Clifton (dau of Gervase Clifton of Clifton)
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Children:'''
2 CONT ** 1 Richard Blount of Iver, Sheriff of Buckinghamshire and Bedfordshire (d 30.11.1508) m. Elizabeth (or Dorothy) de la Ford (dau of William de la Ford of Iver) ___________________________
