2 CONT *'''Walter Blount, 1st Lord Mountjoy1
2 CONT *'''M, #107424, b. circa 1420, d. 1 August 1474
2 CONT *Last Edited=2 Feb 2013
2 CONT *'''     Walter Blount, 1st Lord Mountjoy was born circa 1420.1 He was the son''' of Thomas Blount and Margaret Gresley.2 '''He married, firstly, Helena Byron''', daughter of John Byron and Margery Booth, '''before 1443. He marrie
2 CONC d, secondly, Lady Anne Neville''', daughter of Ralph de Neville, 1st Earl of Westmorland and Lady Joan de Beaufort, '''before 25 November 1467.3 He died on 1 August 1474.3
2 CONT *'''     He gained the title of 1st Lord Mountjoy.1 He lived at Thurveston, Derbyshire, England.
2 CONT *'''Children of Walter Blount, 1st Lord Mountjoy and Helena Byron
2 CONT **Charles Blount
2 CONT **William Blount+ d. 14 Apr 1471
2 CONT **John Blount, 3rd Baron Mountjoy+4 d. 1485
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 109. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families.
2 CONT *[S1916] Tim Boyle, "re: Boyle Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 16 September 2006. Hereinafter cited as "re: Boyle Family."
2 CONT *[S8] BP1999 volume 1, page 17. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S8]
2 CONT *[S22] Sir Bernard Burke, C.B. LL.D., A Genealogical History of the Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited, and Extinct Peerages of the British Empire, new edition (1883; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland: Genealogical Publishing Company, 1978), 
2 CONC page 55. Hereinafter cited as Burkes Extinct Peerage.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p10743.htm#i107424 _______________________
2 CONT *'''Walter BLOUNT (1º B Mountjoy)
2 CONT *'''Born: ABT 1416, Rock, Worcester, England /1420, Barton Blount, Derbyshire, England
2 CONT *'''Died: 1 Aug 1474
2 CONT *'''Buried: 1 Aug 1474, Grey Friars, London, Middlesex, England
2 CONT *'''Notes: Knight of the Garter. Lord High Treasurer. Fought for Edward IV at the battle of Towton.
2 CONT *Father: Thomas BLOUNT (Sir)
2 CONT *Mother: Margery GRESLEY
2 CONT *'''Married 1: Helena (Ellen) BYRON (b. ABT 1416)
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. William BLOUNT (Sir Knight)
2 CONT **2. James BLOUNT (Sir Knight)
2 CONT **3. John BLOUNT (3° B. Mountjoy)
2 CONT **4. Edward BLOUNT
2 CONT **5. Anne BLOUNT
2 CONT **6. Elizabeth BLOUNT
2 CONT *'''Married 2: Anne NEVILLE (D. Buckingham) 25 Nov 1467, Raby, Durham, England
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/BLOUNT1.htm#Walter BLOUNT (1° B. Mountjoy) _____________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 05
2 CONT *'''Blount, Walter (d.1474)'''     by Sidney Lee
2 CONT *'''BLOUNT, WALTER, first Baron Montjoy or Mountjoy (d. 1474), lord high treasurer of England, eldest son''' of Sir Thomas Blount '''and grandson''' of Sir Walter Blount [q. v.], '''became treasurer of Calais in 1460, apparently i
2 CONC n immediate succession to his father; fought bravely with the Yorkists at the decisive battle of Towton (29 March 1461), and was rewarded first by knighthood and afterwards by promotion to the governorship of Calais. In October 14
2 CONC 61 he was besieging with a very large force ‘the Castell of Hampnes by side Cales,’ which apparently held out for Henry VI. In 1465 he was nominated lord high treasurer of England, and was raised to the peerage as Baron Montjoy or
2 CONC  Mountjoy, on 20 June of the same year. In 1467 he was given the Devonshire estates forfeited to the crown by the attainder of Thomas Courtenay, earl of Devonshire, in 1461. He was directed in 1468 to accompany the king in a proje
2 CONC cted expedition to aid the Duke of Brittany against Louis XI. In the following year Mountjoy accompanied Edward IV on his public entry into London after his release from the temporary confinement to which Warwick and Clarence had 
2 CONC subjected him. He was created a knight of the Garter on 23 April 1472.
2 CONT *'''Lord Mountjoy died late in 1474, and was buried in the church of Grey Friars, London. His piety was as far-famed as his bravery. On 17 Sept. 1469 he and his wife were received into the fraternity of the chapter of the Holy Tri
2 CONC nity priory at Canterbury. By his will, dated 8 July 1474, he largely increased the endowment of the hospital of St. Leonards, near Alkmonton, Derbyshire, originally founded by his grandmother''', Donna Sancha de Ayála [see under 
2 CONC Blount, Sir Walter, (d. 1403)], '''and established a chapel in the same village. He was twice married: (1) to Helena, daughter''' of Sir John Byron of Clayton, Lancashire, '''and (2), in 1467, to Ann, widow''' of Humphrey Stafford
2 CONC , duke of Buckingham, and daughter of Ralph Nevill, earl of Westmorland, by Joan Beaufort, only daughter of Catherine Swynford and John of Gaunt. '''By his second wife, who died in 1479, he had no issue. In 1470 Lord Mountjoy was 
2 CONC appointed custodian of the estates of the dukedom of Buckingham during the minority of his stepson''' Henry Stafford, the heir. '''By his first marriage he had three sons'''. William, the eldest, was killed while fighting with Edw
2 CONC ard IV at Barnet in 1471, and was '''buried with his father at Grey Friars'''. William's son Edward '''succeeded his grandfather as the second Baron Mountjoy in 1474''', but died in the following year, and was buried in the Grey F
2 CONC riars' church in London. '''The second son''', Sir John, succeeded his nephew Edward as third Baron Mountjoy in 1475; was appointed captain of Guisnes and Hammes near Calais in 1477; was continued in the office by Richard III in 1
2 CONC 483; died in 1485, bequeathing his body to the Grey Friars' church; and was succeeded in his title by his son William [q. v.] '''The third son''', Sir James, became lieutenant of Hammes in 1476; joined in offering the castle of Ha
2 CONC mmes to Henry, earl of Richmond, in 1484–5; was with Henry VII on his landing at Milford Haven in 1485; was knighted there; became a knight banneret in 1487; and died in 1493 (cf. Polydore Vergil, Camd. Soc. 208, 212).
2 CONT *[Sir Alexander Croke's Genealogical History of the Croke Family, surnamed Le Blount, ii. 197–204; Dugdale's Baronage; Rymer's Fœdera, xi. 504, 578, 630, 656–7, 767; Stow's Survey, ed. Strype, bk. iii. 133; Paston Letters, ed. Gai
2 CONC rdner, ii. 5, 52, 169, 389; Nichols's Leicestershire, iii. 7, iv. 523, 524.]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Blount,_Walter_(d.1474)_(DNB00)
2 CONT * https://archive.org/stream/dictionaryofnati05stepuoft#page/258/mode/1up _______________
2 CONT *'''Sir Walter Blount
2 CONT *'''Birth: 	1416, England
2 CONT *'''Death: 	Aug. 1, 1474, England
2 CONT *'''Lord Montjoy, High Treasurer, Steward of the High Peak in Derbyshire, Treasurer of Calais, 
2 CONT *'''Eldest son''' of Sir Thomas Blount and Margery Gresley. '''Grandson of''' Sir Walter Blount and Sancha de Ayala, '''great grandson of''' Sir Thomas de Mountjoy. 
2 CONT *'''Husband of Helena Byron and father of six'''; Sir William, John, James, Edward, Anne, Elizabeth.
2 CONT *'''Secondly, husband of Ann Neville, widow''' of Sir Humphrey Stafford Duke of Buckingham. 
2 CONT *'''Sir Walter was made a Knight of the Garter in 1472. 
2 CONT *'''On his death, his title went to his grandson''', Edward, whose father, Sir William, had been killed at the Battle of Barnet in 1471.
