2 CONT =Anne de Beauchamp 15th Countess of Warwick=
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_de_Beauchamp,_15th_Countess_of_Warwick from WIkipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anne de Beauchamp, 15th Countess of Warwick''' (February 1443 – 3 January 1448) was born in Cardiff,[citation needed] the only child of Henry de Beauchamp, 1st Duke of Warwick and Lady Cecily Neville. Her maternal grandparents 
2 CONC were Richard Neville, 5th Earl of Salisbury and Alice Montagu, Countess of Salisbury. Anne was niece to Richard Neville, who by marriage to her aunt Lady Anne Beauchamp, inherited the title Earl of Warwick.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandfather was the brother of Cecily Neville, Duchess of York, wife of Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York. Her mother was a first cousin of Edward IV of England, Edmund, Earl of Rutland, Margaret of York, George P
2 CONC lantagenet, 1st Duke of Clarence and Richard III of England.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1445, her father died. While his dukedom became extinct, his earldom was inherited by his daughter, so Anne became the suo jure 15th Countess of Warwick at age two. Anne also succeeded to the title of 7th Baroness Burghersh [E.
2 CONC , 1330] on 11 June 1446, suo jure.[1] However, she died just three years later at Ewelme in Oxfordshire, the home of her maternal step-great-grandmother. She was buried in Reading Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her title as Countess of Warwick was inherited by her paternal aunt, Lady Anne, and maternal uncle, Sir Richard. The barony of Burghersh fell into abeyance between her aunts.[1]
