2 CONT '''     Anne de Holand was born circa 1455.1 She was the daughter of''' Henry de Holand, 2nd Duke of Exeter and Anne Plantagenet.1 '''She married Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset,''' son of Sir John Grey and Elizabeth Wydevill,
2 CONC  '''in October 1466 at Greenwich, London, EnglandG.2 She died in 1475.'''1
2 CONT '''     She was also known as Anne Holland. From October 1466, her married name became Grey.'''
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 134. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families.
2 CONT 2.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume IV, page 418. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p10756.htm#i107560
2 CONT ________
2 CONT Henry Holland, 3rd Duke of Exeter, 3rd Earl of Huntington (27 June 1430 – September 1475) was a Lancastrian leader during the English Wars of the Roses. He was the only son of John Holland, 2nd Duke of Exeter, and his first wife, 
2 CONC Anne Stafford. His maternal grandparents were Edmund Stafford, 5th Earl of Stafford, and Anne of Gloucester.
2 CONT Career
2 CONT He inherited the Dukedom of Exeter and Earl of Huntington when his father died in 1447. However, he was cruel, savagely temperamental and unpredictable, and so had little support. P.M. Kendall describes him as "dangerous", and was
2 CONC  seen as "cruel and fierce" by contemporary Italian observers.[1]
2 CONT Constable of the Tower
2 CONT Exeter was for a time Constable of the Tower of London, and afterwards the rack there came to be called "the Duke of Exeter's daughter".[2]
2 CONT Wars of the Roses
2 CONT In 1447 he married Anne, the eight-year-old daughter of Richard of York. However, in the Wars of the Roses, he remained loyal to Henry VI against the Yorkists. He was imprisoned at Wallingford Castle when York briefly seized power
2 CONC  after the First Battle of St Albans in 1455. In 1458 he participated in The Love Day, an attempt at reconciliation between the rival factions. He was a commander at the Lancastrian victories at the Battle of Wakefield in 1460 and
2 CONC  the Second Battle of St Albans in 1461, and in the decisive defeat at Towton in 1461. He fled to Scotland after the battle, and then joined Queen Margaret in her exile in France. He was attainted in 1461, and his estates were giv
2 CONC en to his wife, who separated from him in 1464. During the brief period of Henry VI's restoration he was able to regain many of his estates and posts.
2 CONT At the Battle of Barnet, Exeter commanded the Lancastrian left flank. He was badly wounded and left for dead, but survived. Afterwards he was imprisoned, and Anne divorced him in 1472. He "volunteered" to serve on Edward IV's 1475
2 CONC  expedition to France. On the return voyage he fell overboard and drowned, his body being found in the sea between Dover and Calais, Fabyan saying "but how he drowned, the certainty is not known".[3] However, Giovanni Panicharolla
2 CONC , the Milanese envoy to the Burgundian court, was told by Duke Charles that the King of England had given specific orders for the sailors to throw his former brother-in-law overboard.[4][5]
2 CONT Family
2 CONT On 30 January 1446, in the chapel of the Bishop of Ely, Hatfield, Herefordshire,[6] he married Anne of York, the eldest child of Richard, Duke of York, and Cecily Neville.[7] She was an older sister of Edward IV and Richard III.
2 CONT He had one legitimate child:[7]
2 CONT *'''Anne Holland (1461[8] – between 26 August 1467 and 6 June 1474), who married Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset.'''
2 CONT Since Henry had no legitimate male issue the disposition of his estates became a complex matter for his widow, the dowager Duchess of Exeter.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Holland,_3rd_Duke_of_Exeter
2 CONT ________
2 CONT '''Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset, 1st Earl of Huntingdon, 7th Baron Ferrers of Groby, KG''' (1455 – 20 September 1501[1][2]) was an English nobleman, courtier and the eldest son of Elizabeth Woodville and her first husband S
2 CONC ir John Grey of Groby. Her second marriage to King Edward IV made her Queen of England, thus elevating Grey's status at court and in the realm as the stepson of the King.[3] Through his mother's endeavours, he made two materially 
2 CONC advantageous marriages to wealthy heiresses, '''the King's niece Anne Holland''' and the King’s cousin, Cecily Bonville, 7th Baroness Harington. By the latter, he had 14 children.
2 CONT Family
2 CONT '''Thomas Grey''' was born in 1455 close to the Palace of Westminster, near the City of London. He was the elder son of John Grey (c.1432-1461) of Groby in Leicestershire, by his wife Elizabeth Woodville, who later became queen co
2 CONC nsort to King Edward IV.[4]
2 CONT Career
2 CONT His mother endeavoured to improve his estates by the conventional methods of their class and time, through his marriages and purchase of wardships. He also found favour with Edward, fighting in the Battle of Tewkesbury in 1471. Gr
2 CONC ey became Lord Harington and Bonville by right of his second wife Cecily Bonville. In 1475 he was created marquess of Dorset, and he was also a knight of the Garter and a privy councillor.[4]
2 CONT On the death of his stepfather, Edward IV, Grey proved unable to maintain his family's position. It was not possible to arrange a Woodville regency. Internal fighting, particularly the long-established battle for ascendancy in Lei
2 CONC cestershire between the Grey and Hastings families, was now on the national stage. Richard III came to the throne when the sons of Edward IV union with Elizabeth Woodville were declared bastards; the Grey family was aligned with E
2 CONC dward.
2 CONT On 25 June 1483, an assembly of Parliament declared Richard III to be the legitimate king, and Thomas's uncle, Anthony Woodville, 2nd Earl Rivers, and brother, Richard Grey, were executed. Later in the summer, learning of the appa
2 CONC rent murder of both his young half-brothers, Grey joined the Duke of Buckingham's rebellion against Richard III. When the rebellion failed he fled to Brittany to join Henry Tudor, who pledged to marry Grey's half-sister Elizabeth 
2 CONC of York and heal the division between the Yorkists and the Lancastrians.
2 CONT However, just before Henry and the Lancastrian army left to launch their ultimately successful invasion of England in August 1485, Grey heard rumours from England that his mother had come to terms with Richard III, and he was pers
2 CONC uaded to desert Henry Tudor. He was intercepted at Compiègne on his way to England, and played no part in the invasion or subsequent overthrow of Richard III. Grey was instead confined to Paris, as security for the repayment of a 
2 CONC loan made to Henry Tudor by the French government, unable to return home until Henry VII was safely installed as king of England.
2 CONT Thereafter Henry VII took good care to keep his half-brother-in-law under control and Grey was not permitted to recover his former influence, although his attainder was reversed. '''Thomas Grey''' was confined in the Tower in 1487
2 CONC  during Lambert Simnel's rising and not released until after the House of Tudor victory at the Battle of Stoke Field. Though he accompanied the King on his expedition to France in 1492, he was obliged to commit himself in writing 
2 CONC to ensure he did not commit treason. He was permitted to assist in the suppression of the Cornish rising in 1497.
2 CONT '''Thomas Grey, Marquess of Dorset''', died in London on 20 September 1501, aged about 46, and was buried in the collegiate church of Astley, Warwickshire. His widow married Grey's cousin, Henry Stafford, later Earl of Wiltshire.
2 CONT Marriages and issue
2 CONT His mother sought to make provision for him by marriage to wealthy heiresses. '''He married firstly, at Greenwich in October 1466, Lady Anne Holland (1461[5] – c. 1474), the only daughter''' of Henry Holland, 3rd Duke of Exeter, a
2 CONC nd Anne of York. His mother-in-law was the second child and eldest surviving daughter of Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York, and Cecily Neville, thus sister to his mother's second husband King Edward IV.
2 CONT '''After Anne Holland died young without issue''', Thomas married secondly, by papal dispensation 5 September 1474,[6] Cecily Bonville, 7th Baroness Harington of Aldingham and 2nd Baroness Bonville, the wealthiest heiress in Engla
2 CONC nd.[7] Cecily Bonville, born in 1461, was the daughter and heiress of William Bonville, 6th Baron Harington, by his wife Katherine Neville, daughter of Richard Neville, 5th Earl of Salisbury.[6] Katherine was sister to the late Ea
2 CONC rl of Warwick and thus aunt to his daughters.
2 CONT By his second wife Grey had seven sons and seven daughters:[6]
2 CONT *Lord Edward Grey, eldest son and heir, who predeceased his father, and was buried in the church of St Clement Danes, London. He married Anne (née Jerningham), daughter of Sir Edward Jerningham (died 6 January 1515) of Somerleyton
2 CONC , Suffolk, by Margaret Bedingfield (died 24 March 1504), by whom he had no issue. After his death she remarried four times, firstly to a husband surnamed Berkeley; secondly to Henry Barley (died 12 November 1529) of Albury, Hertfo
2 CONC rdshire;[8] thirdly to Sir Robert Drury; and fourthly to Sir Edmund Walsingham.[9][10][11][12][3][13]
2 CONT *Anthony Grey, who predeceased his father.
2 CONT *Thomas Grey, 2nd Marquess of Dorset (22 June 1477 – 22 June 1530), who married firstly Eleanor St John, by whom he had no issue, and secondly Margaret Wotton, widow of William Medley, esquire, and daughter of Sir Robert Wotton by
2 CONC  Anne Belknap, daughter of Henry Belknap esquire, by whom he had four sons, including Henry Grey, 1st Duke of Suffolk, father of Lady Jane Grey, and four daughters.[14]
2 CONT *Sir Richard Grey, who married Florence Pudsey. He is mentioned in the will of his brother, Sir John Grey.[6][15]
2 CONT *Sir John Grey, who married firstly Elizabeth Catesby, widow of Roger Wake (died 16 May 1504) of Blisworth, Northamptonshire, and daughter of Sir William Catesby, and secondly Anne Barley or Barlee (died 1557 or 1558), widow of Si
2 CONC r Robert Sheffield of Butterwick, Lincolnshire, Speaker of the House of Commons. Grey apparently had no issue by either of his wives, as his will dated 3 March 1523 makes no mention of children. After Grey's death his widow, Anne,
2 CONC  married Sir Richard Clement (d.1538) of Ightham Mote, Kent.[16][17]
2 CONT *Leonard Grey, 1st Viscount Grane (c. 1490 – 28 June 1541),[18] According to Richardson, Grey married firstly Elizabeth Arundel, widow of Sir Giles Daubeney, and secondly Eleanor Sutton, daughter of Edward Sutton, 2nd Baron Dudley
2 CONC  by Cecily Willoughby, daughter and coheiress of Sir William Willoughby; however according to Lyons it is unclear whether Grey ever married.[16][19][20] He is mentioned in the will of his brother, Sir John Grey.[15] He served as L
2 CONC ord Lieutenant of Ireland.
2 CONT *George Grey, in holy orders. He is mentioned in the will of his brother, Sir John Grey.[6][15]
2 CONT *Cecily Grey (buried 28 April 1554 at St Margaret's, Westminster),[21] who married John Sutton, 3rd Baron Dudley.[16]
2 CONT *Bridget Grey,[6] believed to have died young.
2 CONT *Dorothy Grey, Baroness Montjoy (1480–1552),[22] who married firstly Robert Willoughby, 2nd Baron Willoughby de Broke, by whom she had issue, and secondly William Blount, 4th Baron Mountjoy.[16]
2 CONT *Elizabeth Grey, who married Gerald FitzGerald, 9th Earl of Kildare.[6]
2 CONT *Margaret Grey, who married Richard Wake, esquire,[6] She is mentioned, as 'Margaret Grey', in the will of her brother, Sir John Grey.[6][15]
2 CONT *Eleanor Grey (or "Elizabeth"[23]) Grey (died by December 1503) who married, as his first wife, Sir John Arundell (1474–1545) of Lanherne, Cornwall, Receiver General of the Duchy of Cornwall and "the most important man in the coun
2 CONC ty".[24]
2 CONT *Mary Grey (1493 – 22 February 1538),[25][26] who married Walter Devereux, 1st Viscount Hereford.[6]
2 CONT Titles
2 CONT Baron Astley, from 1461, inherited on the death of his father
2 CONT Earl of Huntingdon, 1471–1475, created for him. Surrendered and given to William Herbert, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, who himself was forced to surrender his own title so that the king could give it to his son, Edward.
2 CONT Lord Harington and Bonville in right of his (second) wife, from 1474, his wife being unable to sit in Parliament
2 CONT Marquess of Dorset, from 1475, created for Thomas Grey on 14 May 1475 (Whitsunday) in place of the re-possessed earldom of Huntingdon
2 CONT Lord Ferrers of Groby, from 1483, inherited on the death of his grandmother Elizabeth Ferrers.
2 CONT Attainted 1484 following the bid to oust Richard III
2 CONT After reversal of his attainder by Henry VII, styled himself marquess of Dorset, lord Ferrers of Groby, Bonville, and Harington
2 CONT Depictions in fiction
2 CONT Thomas Grey, 1st Marquess of Dorset, is depicted in:
2 CONT *Shakespeare's Richard III
2 CONT *The novel The Sunne in Splendour by Sharon Kay Penman
2 CONT Arms
2 CONT The arms of the head of the Grey family are blazoned Barry of six argent and azure in chief three torteaux gules.[citation needed]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Grey,_1st_Marquess_of_Dorset
