1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_of_Middleham
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙  Edward of Middleham  💙
2 CONT
2 CONT .
2 CONT 💙 Edward of Middleham was the only child of King Richard III and his wife Anne Neville.
2 CONT
2 CONT As with many children in the Middle Ages, strict records were not always kept, so there is debate about nearly everything to do with Edward - even his birth date.
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 Edward's birth is reputed to have taken place in the round tower at the south-west corner of the curtain wall of Middleham Castle - traditionally known as the Prince's Tower.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward was most likely born in December 1473, but some sources say he may have been born as late as 1476.
2 CONT
2 CONT This would make him between 7 and 11 years of age when he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 As the nephew of the King, the son of a Duke, and an Earl in his own right, Edward was cared for as befitted his high status. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The prospect of Edward ever becoming King seemed quite unlikely.
2 CONT
2 CONT His uncle was Edward IV the King of England, who also had two heirs - Edward, Prince of Wales and Richard of Shrewsbury, Duke of York.
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 On the death of King Edward IV in 1483, Edward's   father Richard, seized the throne from Edward's young cousin, Edward V.
2 CONT Richard was crowned as Richard III at Westminster Abbey on 6th July 1483. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward did not attend his parents glittering coronation, possibly due to his poor health.
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 Little is known about Edward's life prior to his father's accession to the throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 24th August 1483, Richard III created Edward the Prince of Wales - the traditional title given to the monarch's eldest son and heir.
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 Edward was invested in a lavish ceremony at York.
2 CONT King Richard himself girded his young son with a sword, placed a gold ring on his finger, and a golden staff in his hand.
2 CONT
2 CONT A sumptuous four hour long banquet followed, and Edward walked through the streets of York with his parents, to much joy and cheering.
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 King Richard and Queen Anne were at Nottingham castle, when they were told that Edward had died on the 9th April 1484.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coincidentally, it was the anniversary of the death of Edward's uncle, King Edward IV. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Many in that superstitious age were inclined to interpret the child's death on that very date was divine retribution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard's implication in the usurpation and subsequent disappearance of the young sons of his brother, led to his own son's demise....
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 There is a lot of speculation concerning the whereabouts of Prince Edward’s remains.
2 CONT No surviving record exists of his burial place.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was said that King Richard buried his son, in a secret location. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 A cenotaph which was long thought to be for Edward of Middleham, stands in the north-east corner of the medieval church of St. Helen and Holy Cross, in the Yorkshire village of Sheriff Hutton. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The cenotaph was tentatively identified as that of Edward, partly based on the presence of a small piece of fifteenth-century stained glass set into the window above the tomb. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The glass depicts the 'Sun in Splendour,' a symbol used by the House of York. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 According to one story, Edward was buried in the church.
2 CONT However, it's not beneath where the cenotaph now stands - but on the opposite southern side of the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT This houses the ancestral chapel of the Nevilles, his mother's family. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 💙 King Richard’s hopes of producing another heir were dashed when his wife became ill during the winter season of 1484. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tragically, 11 months after the death of her son, Queen  Anne died at the Palace of Westminster on 16th March 1485. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was only 28 years old and had been Queen Consort of England for almost 2 years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tudor Intruders (and more) 
2 CONT
2 CONT .
2 CONT 💙 Sources - englishmonarchs/plantagenet.
2 CONT .
2 CONT 💙 The cenotaph thought to be for Edward of Middleham, in the medieval church of St. Helen and Holy Cross, in the Yorkshire village of Sheriff Hutton.
2 CONT
