1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Leonor de Trastámara (¿?, Castilla, 1400 - Toledo, Castilla; 1445). Infanta de Aragón e hija de Fernando I de Aragón y de su esposa Leonor de Alburquerque. Leonor fue reina de Portugal (1433 - 1438), como consorte 
2 CONC de Eduardo I, y regente del mismo reino (1438 - 1439).
2 CONT
2 CONT Fue desposada en 1428, con Eduardo I. Tuvo de este matrimonio nueve hijos:
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan (1429 - 1433); 
2 CONT
2 CONT Filipa (1430 - 1439); 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso el Africano (1432 - 1481), rey de Portugal con el nombre de Alfonso V; 
2 CONT
2 CONT María (1432); 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fernando (1433 - 1470), duque de Viseu y padre del rey Manuel I de Portugal; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor (1434 - 1467), emperatriz germánica por su matrimonio con Federico III de Habsburgo; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eduardo (1435); 
2 CONT
2 CONT Catalina (1436 - 1463), religiosa; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Juana (1439 - 1475), reina de Castilla por su matrimonio con Enrique IV. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A la muerte de su esposo (1438), por expreso pedido de este, fue regente de su hijo Alfonso V, pero por su condición de extranjera tuvo que ceder la regencia al año siguiente a su cuñado, el infante Pedro, duque de Coimbra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obtenido de "http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonor_de_Arag%C3%B3n"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT When her husband died in September 13, 1438, she was appointed as regent of Portugal in his will. However, she was inexperienced and, as an Aragonese, unpopular with the people, who preferred the late king's brother Pedro, Duke of
2 CONC  Coimbra. Negotiations for a compromise arrangement were drawn out over several months, but were complicated by the interference of the Count of Barcelos and the Archbishop of Lisbon, as also by her giving birth to a posthumous da
2 CONC ughter in March 1439, and by the death of her eldest daughter, Philippa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, the Cortes appointed Pedro the sole regent. Eleanor continued conspiring, but was forced to go into exile in Castile in December 1440. She died at Toledo in February 1445 and is buried in Batalha, [Portugal]].
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    * Prince John, October 1429-August 14, 1433.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Philippa November 27, 1430-March 24, 1439.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Afonso V of Portugal January 15, 1432-August 28, 1481; who succeeded Duarte as 12th King of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Maria, December 7, 1432-December 8, 1432.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prince Ferdinand, November 17, 1433-September 18, 1470; Duke of Viseu and father of future king Manuel I of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Leonor, September 18, 1434-September 3, 1467; married Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prince Edward, July 12, 1435-July 12, 1435.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Catherine, November 26, 1436-June 17, 1463.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Joana, March 31, 1439-June 13, 1475; married King Henry IV of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonor of Aragon (1402-1445)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Aragon (c. 1402 - February 19, 1445) was the daughter of Ferdinand I of Aragon and Eleanor of Alburquerque.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Duarte (Edward), King of Portugal on September 22, 1428. They had nine children, of which five survived to adulthood.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince John, October 1429-August 14, 1433.
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Philippa November 27, 1430-March 24, 1439.
2 CONT
2 CONT Afonso V of Portugal January 15, 1432-August 28, 1481; who succeeded Duarte as 12th King of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Maria, December 7, 1432-December 8, 1432.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Ferdinand, November 17, 1433-September 18, 1470; Duke of Viseu and father of future king Manuel I of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Leonor, September 18, 1434-September 3, 1467; married Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Edward, July 12, 1435-July 12, 1435.
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Catherine, November 26, 1436-June 17, 1463.
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Joana, March 31, 1439-June 13, 1475; married King Henry IV of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regent
2 CONT
2 CONT When her husband died in September 13, 1438, she was appointed as regent of Portugal in his will. However, she was inexperienced and, as an Aragonese, unpopular with the people, who preferred the late king's brother Pedro, Duke of
2 CONC  Coimbra. Negotiations for a compromise arrangement were drawn out over several months, but were complicated by the interference of the Count of Barcelos and the Archbishop of Lisbon, as also by her giving birth to a posthumous da
2 CONC ughter in March 1439, and by the death of her eldest daughter, Philippa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, the Cortes appointed Pedro the sole regent. Eleanor continued conspiring, but was forced to go into exile in Castile in December 1440. She died at Toledo in February 1445 and is buried in Batalha, Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eleanor of Aragon (1 February 1402 – 19 February 1445) was queen consort of Portugal as the spouse of Edward I of Portugal and the regent of Portugal as the guardian of her son. She was the daughter of Ferdinand I of Aragon and El
2 CONC eanor of Alburquerque.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT She married King Edward of Portugal on 22 September 1428. They had nine children, of whom five survived to adulthood.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her husband died on 13 September 1438, she was appointed regent of Portugal in his will, which was confirmed by the Portuguese Cortes. However, she was inexperienced and, as an Aragonese, unpopular with the people, who prefer
2 CONC red the late king's brother Infante Peter, Duke of Coimbra. The confirmation of her regency therefore caused a riot in Lisbon. The riot was suppressed by her brother Count John of Barcelona, later King John II of Aragon. Eleanor w
2 CONC as supported by the nobility and the will, while Peter was supported by a fraction of the nobility and by the people. Negotiations for a compromise arrangement were drawn out over several months, but were complicated by the interf
2 CONC erence of the Count of Barcelos, who supported her, and the Archbishop of Lisbon, who supported Peter. This period also witnessed the birth to her of a posthumous daughter, Joan in March 1439 and the death of her eldest daughter, 
2 CONC Philippa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, the Cortes appointed Peter the sole regent. Eleanor continued conspiring, but was forced to go into exile in Castile in December 1440. She died at Toledo in February 1445 and is buried in Batalha, Portugal. Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     Infante John, October 1429 – 14 August 1433.
2 CONT     Infanta Philippa 27 November 1430 – 24 March 1439.
2 CONT     Afonso V of Portugal 15 January 1432 – 28 August 1481; who succeeded Edward as 12th King of Portugal.
2 CONT     Infanta Maria, 7 December 1432 – 8 December 1432.
2 CONT     Infante Ferdinand, 17 November 1433 – 18 September 1470; Duke of Viseu and father of future king Manuel I of Portugal.
2 CONT     Eleanor, 18 September 1434 – 3 September 1467; married Frederick III, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT     Infante Edward, 12 July 1435 – 12 July 1435.
2 CONT     Catherine, 26 November 1436 – 17 June 1463.
2 CONT     Joan, 31 March 1439 – 13 June 1475; married King Henry IV of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestry
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleanor_of_Aragon,_Queen_of_Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Aragon (1 February 1402 – 19 February 1445) was queen consort of Portugal as the spouse of Edward I of Portugal and the regent of Portugal as the guardian of her son. She was the daughter of Ferdinand I of Aragon and El
2 CONC eanor of Alburquerque. Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT She married King Edward of Portugal on 22 September 1428. They had nine children, of whom five survived to adulthood.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her husband died on 13 September 1438, she was appointed regent of Portugal in his will, which was confirmed by the Portuguese Cortes. However, she was inexperienced and, as an Aragonese, unpopular with the people, who prefer
2 CONC red the late king's brother Infante Peter, Duke of Coimbra. The confirmation of her regency therefore caused a riot in Lisbon. The riot was suppressed by her brother Count John of Barcelona, later King John II of Aragon. Eleanor w
2 CONC as supported by the nobility and the will, while Peter was supported by a fraction of the nobility and by the people. Negotiations for a compromise arrangement were drawn out over several months, but were complicated by the interf
2 CONC erence of the Count of Barcelos, who supported her, and the Archbishop of Lisbon, who supported Peter. This period also witnessed the birth to her of a posthumous daughter, Joan in March 1439 and the death of her eldest daughter, 
2 CONC Philippa.
