1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jacqueline de Montbel d'Entremont (1541–1588), the Countess d'Entremont and Launay-Gelin, Countess of d'Entremont and of Nottage, heiress of the branch. Jacqueline de Montbel was the last of the direct name. The Mo
2 CONC ntbel d'Entremont family is thus extinguished in the direct line.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Summary
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/236447040/jacqueline_de_montbel_d&#39;entremont
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline de Montbel d'Entremont was Countess of Entremont and Montbel, Maid of Honour of Princess Marguerite of France. She obtained the erection with the barony of Montelier in Bresse into a marquisate with the first and second
2 CONC  degree of jurisdiction, with the sole reservation of sovereignty and jurisdiction from Charles-Emmanuel of Savoy, who married her in 1571 to Gaspard II de Coligny , Admiral of France, leader of the Protestants, who was assassinat
2 CONC ed during the Saint-Barthélemy Massacre on 23 Aug 1572. The admiral's widow took refuge in the Château de Saint-André in 1572 and gave birth there to a daughter, Béatrix de Coligny, born 21 Dec 1572.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline died in prison in the Ivrea Castle in Piedmont.
2 CONT
2 CONT C.Smith
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Taken from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacqueline_de_Montbel_d%27Entremont (which may have errors)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jacqueline de Montbel d'Entremont''' (16 February 1541 – 17 December 1599) was a French courtier, possible artistic muse and Huguenot, known for her experiences during the French wars of religion. After her first husband's deat
2 CONC h, she converted to Protestantism and married Gaspard II de Coligny, who was later killed in the St. Bartholomew's Day Massacre. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Jacqueline was the only daughter and sole heir to the Montbel d’Entremont family, which held several fiefdoms in Savoy, Bresse, Bugey, and Piedmont.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother was Countess Beatrix d’Entremont, her father was Count Jean of Sifuente and Duke of Ascalana. She grew up in the Louvre for 6 years, where her mother was a maid of honor to Queen Eleanor of Austria. When Queen Eleanor t
2 CONC ook refuge in the Spanish Netherlands in 1547, Jacqueline went with her mother to the Chateau de Saint-André at Briord in Bugey. She remained in the royal court in Brussels until age sixteen, where she was then chosen to be a maid
2 CONC  of honor to princess Marguerite of France, where she returned to the Louvre. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She married 
2 CONT # Claude de Bastarnay, the nephew of Diane de Poitiers, on 16 February 1561.  He was killed at the Battle of Saint-Denis, where the Protestant forces were led by Jacqueline's future second husband Gaspard II de Coligny (10 Novembe
2 CONC r 1567).  No children.
2 CONT # 	Gaspard II de Coligny,  seigneur de Châtillon, admiral of France (16 February 1519 – 24 August 1572); his second wife; he was Charlotte Laval's widower.  They had one daughter born after his assassination:
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT #Béatrix, born on  21 December 1572, who became Beatrice de Coligny (born 1572), Countess d'Entremont by marriage to Claude-Antoine d'Albon de Montauban de Meuillon d'Entremont.  Died 1623.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Furious at this union, Emmanuel-Philibert of Savoy confiscated Jacqueline de Montbel's immense fortune. Gaspard II de Coligny then sought to get back into the good graces of Charles IX of France, who had sentenced him to death an
2 CONC d had his property confiscated.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT According to Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1575 and 1594 Jacqueline was once again officially dowager countess of Montbel d'Entremont and resumed her life at the court in Turin. She took part in all the festivities and intrigues and gave birth to an illegitimate da
2 CONC ughter in great secrecy in February 1578 - she was christened Marguerite after Margaret, the late duchess of Savoy. The father was the Duke of Savoy and the child was adopted by Cassandre, countess of None and sister of André Prov
2 CONC ana de Leyni. The secret was so well guarded that it was only discovered in the 19th century - in 1870 the historian Victor de Saint-Genis argued that against its historicity, but fourteen years later this was proven by Gaudenzio 
2 CONC Claretta, a historian from Turin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Duke died on 30 August 1580 and his son and successor Charles Emmanuel I redressed many issues with the neighbouring nations of France and Geneva. Intrigues continued at the ducal court in Turin and Jacqueline was strongly sus
2 CONC pected of secretly negotiating with Henry IV of France after his conversion to Catholicism and his coronation at Chartres on 27 February 1594. The Holy Office had been kept informed of Jacqueline's affair and when Marguerite was s
2 CONC uspected of being possessed by a demon it accused Jacqueline of witchcraft.[12] Cardinal d'Ossat defended her, but she was imprisoned in the Moncalieri fortress in Turin. She was cleared of sorcery but kept in prison on new charge
2 CONC s of treason.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in prison until her death, 17 December 1599, while imprisoned in the castle at Ivrea. According to Ossat she seems to have received the Catholic last rites, though her place of burial is unknown. Only thirteen years later
2 CONC , in 1601, Charles Emmanuel I was forced to cede Bresse, Bugey le Valromey and the country of Gex once and for all to Henry IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacqueline_de_Montbel_d%27Entremont
2 CONT Enfant: '''Beatrix de Coligny, Comtesse d'Entremont ''' (d) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Succession
2 CONT
2 CONT *Béatrice de Coligny, (Daughter of Jacqueline de Montbel d'Entremont), Countess of Montbel d'Entremont, (1572-…), married the November 30, 1600 Claude-Antoine Bon, Lord Baron of Meuillon. (…-1618), to whom the fiefs and the name o
2 CONC f Montbel passed.
2 CONT **François Bon, known as d'Albon, Count of Montbel and d'Entremont, married Madeleine du Tillet. (François Virginie Bon, comte d'Entremont et de Montbel (1601 - 1671).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline de Montbel d'Entremont letter to Paolo Sfondrati  starts at page 3 : It is written in Spanish, would be wonderful if someone could translate it into English.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://cdm15999.contentdm.oclc.org/cdm/ref/collection/SCMisc/id/53813
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacqueline_de_Montbel_d%27Entremont
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia only has a French version written about her, wish someone could translate it into English. [Comment: that's been done]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline Montbel of Entremont was born February 16, 1541 in Paris, in a house near the Palace of the Louvre1. She died in prison in the castle of Ivrea (Piedmont) on 17 December 1599. Widow in first marriage, she converted to Pr
2 CONC otestantism and she married in second marriage Admiral Gaspard II de Coligny, which it will be widowed during the massacre of St. Bartholomew.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Is this proven?
2 CONT
2 CONT From the profile for https://www.geni.com/people/Gaspard-II-de-Coligny-comte-de-Coligny/6000000003151508258
2 CONT
2 CONT >After the Admiral's death, his wife, the Countess felt indebted to the children of https://www.geni.com/people/Nicolas-Mius/6000000002447118827. She took under her protection a son of Nicolas. (Claude Mius Demeuion d’Entremont.) 
2 CONC The boy was actually placed under the care of Jacqueline's mother, the Countess Beatrice Bacheoc-d'Entremont of the House of Montbel d'Entremont of Savioe. The name of d'Entremont was then added to his surname Mius. This was Jacqu
2 CONC eline's wish, since she was the only child who survived her parents’ marriage, and had only a daughter  from her marriage to the Admiral. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://joedentremont.com/family-history-1500s-a-d/#countess-beatrix
2 CONT
2 CONT >Jacqueline also took Nicolas Mius and Jeanne Mius de Meuillon’s children, and adopted them, as Nicolas Mius was murdered along with Gaspard in the massacre. She took Claude Antoine Mius, and sent him to her mother, Beatrix, to ra
2 CONC ise in her care. She then sent Nicolas’s daughters Charlotte Mius d’Entremont and Louyse Mius d’Entremont into the care of her stepdaughter Louise de Coligny, Princess of Orange, who was to raise them. After all of this, Jacquelin
2 CONC e then signed documents to which all of Nicolas Mius’ descendents, regardless male or female, would carry the name and coat of arms of Count d’Entremont – her father. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
