0 @N6000000002188376382@ NOTE John II of Castile
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John II (March 6, 1405 – July 20, 1454) was King of Castile from 1406 to 1454. He was the son of Henry III of Castile and his wife Katherine of Lancaster, daughter of John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster by Constance of Castile, daugh
1 CONC ter of King Pedro of Castile (known as 'Pedro the Cruel'). He was thus great-grandson of King Edward III of England through the maternal line.
1 CONT Castilian and Leonese royalty
1 CONT House of Trastámara
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry II and I of Leon
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    John I
1 CONT    Eleanor, Queen of Navarre
1 CONT John I
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    Henry III of Castile and II of Leon)
1 CONT    Ferdinand I of Aragon, Valencia and Sicily
1 CONT Henry III and II of Leon
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    John II
1 CONT    Maria, Queen of Aragon, Valencia, Sicily and Naples
1 CONT John II
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    Henry IV of Castile and III of Leon
1 CONT    Isabella I
1 CONT    Alfonso, Prince of Asturias
1 CONT Henry IV and III of Leon
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Joan, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT Isabella I with Ferdinand V
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Isabella, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT    Juan, Prince of Asturias
1 CONT    Joanna I
1 CONT    Maria, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT    Catherine, Queen of England
1 CONT Joanna
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded his father on December 25, 1406, at the age of a year and ten months, and united in his person the claims of Pedro the Cruel and of Henry of Trastamara. His mother Katherine and his uncle, Ferdinand, were co-regents d
1 CONC uring his minority. When Ferdinand died in 1416, his mother was sole regent until she died as well, in 1418.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was one of the many misfortunes of Castile that the long reign of John II—forty-nine years—should have been granted to one of the least capable of her kings. Juan was amiable, weak, and dependent on those about him. He had no t
1 CONC aste except for ornament and no serious interest except in amusements such as verse-making, hunting, and tournaments.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was entirely under the influence of his favourite, Álvaro de Luna, until his second wife, Isabella of Portugal, obtained control of his feeble will. At her instigation, he dismissed his faithful and able favorite, an act which 
1 CONC is said to have caused him much remorse. He died on July 20, 1454, at Valladolid.
1 CONT
1 CONT John II of Castile declared the Valladolid laws, which restricted the social rights of Jews. Among many other restrictions the laws forced Jews to wear distinctive clothes and denied from them any administrative positions.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Ancestors
1 CONT John's ancestors in three generations John II of Castile Father:
1 CONT Henry III of Castile Paternal Grandfather:
1 CONT John I of Castile Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Henry II of Castile
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Juana Manuel of Castile
1 CONT Paternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Eleanor of Aragon Paternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Peter IV of Aragon
1 CONT Paternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Eleanor of Sicily
1 CONT Mother:
1 CONT Katherine of Lancaster Maternal Grandfather:
1 CONT John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Edward III of England
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT Philippa of Hainault
1 CONT Maternal Grandmother:
1 CONT Constance of Castile Maternal Great-grandfather:
1 CONT Peter of Castile
1 CONT Maternal Great-grandmother:
1 CONT María de Padilla
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1418, John married his cousin Maria of Aragon, daughter of Ferdinand I of Aragon and Eleanor of Alburquerque. The marriage produced four children but only one son, the future Henry IV of Castile, survived infancy. John was wido
1 CONC wed in 1445 and remarried to Isabel de Portugal, daughter of Infante João of Portugal, with whom he had two children: the future Queen Isabella of Castile and the Infante Alfonso (1453-1468).
1 CONT
