0 @N6000000002188376291@ NOTE Henry IV of Castile
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry IV of Castile
1 CONT Reign
1 CONT
1 CONT Regent 1454-1474
1 CONT Predecessor John II of Castile
1 CONT Successor Isabella of Castile
1 CONT Consort Blanca of Navarre
1 CONT Joan of Portugal
1 CONT Issue
1 CONT Joanna La Beltraneja
1 CONT Royal house House of Trastámara
1 CONT Father John II
1 CONT Mother Isabella of Portugal
1 CONT Born January 5, 1425
1 CONT Valladolid, Spain
1 CONT Died December 11, 1474
1 CONT Madrid, Spain
1 CONT Burial Santa María de Guadalupe, La Mancha, Spain
1 CONT
1 CONT Enrique IV (5 January 1425 - 11 December 1474), King of Castile, nicknamed the Impotent (ruled 1454-1474), was the last of the weak late medieval kings of Castile. During Enrique's reign the nobles increased in power and the natio
1 CONC n became less centralised. He was born in 1425 and was the son of John II of Castile and Maria of Aragon, daughter of King Ferdinand I of Aragon.
1 CONT Henry IV of Castile
1 CONT
1 CONT At the age of fifteen he was married to Blanca of Navarre (1420-1464). Blanca was considered to have remained a virgin, not only on her wedding night, but for the rest of her marriage. After thirteen years, Enrique sought a divorc
1 CONC e. An official examination confirmed the virginity of Blanca, and a priest questioned the prostitutes of Segovia, who confirmed that Henry was sexually capable. Blanca was sent home, eight years later became Queen of Navarre, then
1 CONC , her family imprisoned her, and she was later killed under strange circumstances in 1464 (supposedly, poisoned, but this has not been confirmed).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1455, Enrique married Princess Juana, sister of Afonso V of Portugal. After six years of marriage, in 1462, she gave birth to a daughter, Joanna, also Juana, nicknamed "La Beltraneja". Six years after the birth of the throne's 
1 CONC heir, part of the nobility of Castile revolted against the king. The rebels claimed that the princess was not the daughter of the king, but actually the daughter of Beltrán de La Cueva, 1st Duke of Alburquerque. This hypothesis wa
1 CONC s reinforced when the Queen had another child with the nephew of a Bishop. It is probable that the father of the first daughter of the Queen was the King, but is almost sure that the second one was not the King's. Townsend Miller 
1 CONC maintains that the question of her paternity cannot be answered. However, contemporary Historians assure Henry was impotent.
1 CONT
1 CONT The doubt of her legitimacy as an heir, the weakness of the king, the adultery of the queen, and the unruliness of the nobility all set the stage for a struggle for succession after Enrique's death. Enrique divorced his wife after
1 CONC  her scandalous behavior with Bishop Fonseca's nephew. After a long period of conflict between the rival factions, Enrique finally agreed to name Isabella his successor, in Guisando (Avila), provided she allow him to arrange her s
1 CONC trategic marriage. Isabella would go on to break this stipulation of the agreement.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1474 Henry IV of Castile was entombed at Santa María de Guadalupe, next to his mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of the king war started in Castile. Juana was supported by Portugal, while the eventual winner, his half-sister Isabella of Castile had the support of Aragon via her husband Ferdinand and, later in the war, France.
1 CONC  Today, the succession of the Crown of Castile is still a polemic topic between historians.
1 CONT
