0 @N6000000002188309551@ NOTE Richard III of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard III
1 CONT King of England; Lord of Ireland (more...)
1 CONT Reign 20 June 1483 – 22 August 1485
1 CONT Coronation 6 July 1483
1 CONT Predecessor Edward V
1 CONT Successor Henry VII
1 CONT Consort Anne Neville
1 CONT Issue
1 CONT Edward of Middleham, Prince of Wales
1 CONT Detail
1 CONT Titles and styles
1 CONT The King
1 CONT The Duke of Gloucester
1 CONT Lord Richard Plantagenet
1 CONT Royal house House of York
1 CONT Father Richard, 3rd Duke of York
1 CONT Mother Cecily Neville
1 CONT Born 2 October 1452(1452-10-02)
1 CONT Fotheringhay Castle, Northamptonshire
1 CONT Died 22 August 1485 (aged 32)
1 CONT Bosworth Field, Leicestershire
1 CONT Burial Greyfriars Abbey, Leicestershire[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard III (2 October 1452 – 22 August 1485) was King of England from 1483 until his death. He was the last king from the House of York, and his defeat at the Battle of Bosworth marked the culmination of the Wars of the Roses and
1 CONC  the end of the Plantagenet dynasty. After the death of his brother King Edward IV, Richard briefly governed as regent for Edward's son King Edward V with the title of Lord Protector, but he placed Edward and his brother Richard i
1 CONC n the Tower (see Princes in the Tower) and seized the throne for himself, being crowned on 6 July 1483.
1 CONT
1 CONT Two large-scale rebellions rose against Richard. The first, in 1483, was led by die-hard opponents of Edward IV and, most notably, Richard's own 'kingmaker', Henry Stafford, 2nd Duke of Buckingham. The revolt collapsed and Bucking
1 CONC ham was executed at Salisbury, near the Bull's Head Inn. However, in 1485, another rebellion arose against Richard, headed by Henry Tudor, 2nd Earl of Richmond (later King Henry VII) and his uncle Jasper. The rebels landed troops 
1 CONC and Richard fell in the Battle of Bosworth Field, then known as Redemore or Dadlington Field, as the last Plantagenet king and the last English king to die in battle.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Childhood
1 CONT     * 2 Reign of Edward IV
1 CONT     * 3 Accession to the Throne
1 CONT     * 4 Death at the Battle of Bosworth
1 CONT     * 5 Succession
1 CONT     * 6 Legacy
1 CONT     * 7 Controversy and reputation
1 CONT     * 8 Ancestors
1 CONT     * 9 See also
1 CONT     * 10 Bibliography
1 CONT     * 11 References
1 CONT     * 12 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Childhood
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was born at Fotheringhay Castle, the eighth and youngest, and fourth surviving, son of Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York (who had been a strong claimant to the throne of King Henry VI) and Cecily Neville. Richard spent
1 CONC  much of his childhood at Middleham Castle in Wensleydale, under the tutelage of his cousin Richard Neville, 16th Earl of Warwick (known to history as "The Kingmaker" because of his strong influence on the course of the Wars of th
1 CONC e Roses).
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of the death of his father and older brother Edmund at the Battle of Wakefield, Richard, who was still a boy, was taken into the care of Warwick. While Richard was at Warwick's estate, he developed a close friendship w
1 CONC ith Francis Lovell, a friendship that would remain strong for the rest of his life. Another child in the household was Warwick's daughter Anne Neville, whom Richard would later marry.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reign of Edward IV
1 CONT
1 CONT During the reign of his brother, King Edward IV, Richard demonstrated his loyalty and skill as a military commander. He was rewarded with large estates in northern England, awarded the title Duke of Gloucester and appointed as Gov
1 CONC ernor of the North, becoming the richest and most powerful noble in England and a loyal aide to Edward IV. In contrast, the other surviving brother, George Plantagenet, 1st Duke of Clarence, was executed by Edward for treason.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard controlled the north of England until Edward IV's death. In 1482 Richard recaptured Berwick-upon-Tweed from the Scots, and his administration was regarded as being fair and just, endowing universities and making grants to 
1 CONC the church.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Accession to the Throne
1 CONT Edmund of Langley, 1st Duke of York
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Edward of Norwich, 2nd Duke of York
1 CONT    Richard of Conisburgh, Earl of Cambridge
1 CONT    Constance, Countess of Gloucester
1 CONT Edward of Norwich, 2nd Duke of York
1 CONT Richard of Conisburgh, Earl of Cambridge
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Isabel, Countess of Essex
1 CONT    Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York
1 CONT Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Anne, Duchess of Exeter
1 CONT    Edward IV of England
1 CONT    Edmund, Earl of Rutland
1 CONT    Elizabeth, Duchess of Suffolk
1 CONT    Margaret, Duchess of Burgundy
1 CONT    George, Duke of Clarence
1 CONT    Richard III of England
1 CONT Edward IV of England
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Elizabeth, Queen Consort of England
1 CONT    Mary of York
1 CONT    Cecily Kymbe
1 CONT    Edward V of England
1 CONT    Margaret of York
1 CONT    Richard of Shrewsbury, Duke of York
1 CONT    Anne, Countess of Surrey
1 CONT    George, Duke of Bedford
1 CONT    Catherine, Countess of Devon
1 CONT    Bridget of York
1 CONT George, Duke of Clarence
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Margaret, Countess of Salisbury
1 CONT    Edward, Earl of Warwick
1 CONT Richard III of England
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Edward of Middleham, Prince of Wales
1 CONT
1 CONT On the death of Edward IV, on 9 April 1483, the late King's sons (Richard's young nephews), King Edward V, aged 12, and Richard of Shrewsbury, 1st Duke of York, aged 9, were next in the order of succession. Richard, however, had t
1 CONC he king's guardian, Anthony Woodville, 2nd Earl Rivers (brother of Elizabeth Woodville, Edward IV's Queen Consort) and other advisors arrested and taken to Pontefract Castle, where they were later executed, allegedly for planning 
1 CONC to assassinate Edward V. He then took Edward and his younger brother to the Tower of London.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 22 June 1483, outside St Paul's Cathedral, a statement was read out on behalf of Richard declaring for the first time that he was taking the throne for himself on the grounds that Edward IV's marriage had been illegitimate and 
1 CONC that, in consequence, the true heir to the throne was Richard and not Edward V. This proclamation was then supported by a bill passed by Parliament on the evidence of a bishop who testified to having married Edward to Lady Eleanor
1 CONC  Butler, who was still living when Edward married Elizabeth Woodville.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 6 July 1483, Richard was crowned at Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Richard III is popularly supposed to have killed Edward V and his brother, there is some controversy among historians about the actual circumstances of the boys' deaths: see Princes in the Tower for full coverage, and pos
1 CONC sible reasons for the support for Richard's accession.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death at the Battle of Bosworth
1 CONT
1 CONT On 22 August 1485, Richard met the Lancastrian forces of Henry Tudor at the Battle of Bosworth Field. During the battle Richard was abandoned by Lord Thomas Stanley, 1st Earl of Derby, Sir William Stanley, and Henry Percy, 4th Ear
1 CONC l of Northumberland. The switching of sides by the Stanleys severely depleted the strength of Richard's army and had a material effect on the outcome of the battle. Accounts note that Richard fought bravely and ably during the bat
1 CONC tle, unhorsing a well-known champion, killing Henry's standard bearer and nearly reaching Henry himself before being finally surrounded and killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard's naked body was then paraded through the streets before being buried at Greyfriars Church, Leicester. According to one tradition, during the Dissolution of the Monasteries his body was thrown into the nearby River Soar, a
1 CONC lthough other evidence suggests that this may not be the case and that his burial site may currently be under a car park in Leicester. There is currently a memorial plaque on the site of the Cathedral where he may have once been b
1 CONC uried.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to another tradition, Richard consulted a seer in the town of Leicester before the battle and the seer foretold that "where your spur should strike on the ride into battle, your head shall be broken on the return." On th
1 CONC e ride into battle his spur struck the bridge stone of the Bow Bridge; legend has it that, as his corpse was being carried from the battle over the back of a horse, his head struck the same stone and was broken open [1].
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry Tudor succeeded Richard to become Henry VII, and cemented the succession by marrying the Yorkist heiress, Elizabeth of York, daughter of Edward IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Succession
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the decisive Yorkist victory over the Lancastrians at the Battle of Tewkesbury, Richard had married the younger daughter of the Earl of Warwick, Anne Neville on 12 July 1472. Anne's first husband had been Edward of Westm
1 CONC inster (d 1471), son of Henry VI.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard and Anne had one son, Edward Plantagenet (also known as Edward of Middleham, 1473 – 9 April 1484), who died not long after being created Prince of Wales. Richard also had a number of illegitimate children, including John o
1 CONC f Gloucester and a daughter named Katharine who married William Herbert, 2nd Earl of Pembroke. It has been thought that their mother may have been one Katherine Haute, who is mentioned in household records. Both of these children 
1 CONC survived Richard. Neither apparently left any descendant. The mysterious Richard Plantagenet (Richard of Eastwell) is also a possible offspring of Richard III.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of his last stand against the Lancastrians, Richard was a widower without a legitimate son. After his son's death, he had initially named his nephew, Edward, Earl of Warwick, Clarence's young son and the nephew of Quee
1 CONC n Anne Neville, as his heir. After Anne's death, however, Richard named as his heir another nephew, John de la Pole, Earl of Lincoln, the son of his older sister Elizabeth.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard's death at Bosworth resulted in the end of the Plantagenet dynasty, which had ruled England since the succession of Henry II in 1154. The last male Plantagenet, Edward, Earl of Warwick (son of Richard III's brother Clarenc
1 CONC e) was executed by Henry VII in 1499.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard's Council of the North greatly improved conditions for northern England, as commoners of that region were formerly without any substantial economic activity independent of London. Its descendant position was Secretary of S
1 CONC tate for the Northern Department.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Controversy and reputation
1 CONT
1 CONT Much that was previously considered 'fact' about Richard III has been rejected by modern historians. For example, Richard was represented by Tudor writers as being physically deformed, which was regarded as evidence of an evil cha
1 CONC racter. However, the withered arm, limp and crooked back of legend are nowadays believed to be fabrications, possibly originating from the questionable history attributed to Thomas More, which made a deep impression upon William S
1 CONC hakespeare, and was long taken as the authoritative history of events. The accusations against his moral character have proven more resistant to refutation than the slanders against his physical looks.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Richard III Society was established in the 20th century and has gathered considerable research material about his life and reign. Its aim is summed up by its Patron, the present Richard, Duke of Gloucester:
1 CONT
1 CONT "… the purpose and indeed the strength of the Richard III Society derive from the belief that the truth is more powerful than lies - a faith that even after all these centuries the truth is important. It is proof of our sense of c
1 CONC ivilised values that something as esoteric and as fragile as reputation is worth campaigning for."
1 CONT
1 CONT The American Branch of the Richard III Society carries out its own review of all the suspects in the case of Richard III, in the on-line library "Whodunit?".[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Society of Friends of King Richard III was also set up in the 20th century in order to rehabilitate Richard and to honour his memory. The society is based in the city of York, where following his death in 1485 it was proclaime
1 CONC d, that "King Richard, late reigning mercifully over us, was.... piteously slain and murdered, to the great heaviness of this city".
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard III was found not guilty in a mock trial presided over by three Justices of the United States Supreme Court in 1997. Chief Justice William H. Rehnquist and Associate Justices Ruth Bader Ginsberg and Stephen G. Breyer, in a
1 CONC  3-0 decision, ruled that the prosecution had not met the burden of proof that "it was more likely than not" that the Princes in the Tower had been murdered; that the bones found in 1674 in the Tower were those of the Princes; and
1 CONC  that Richard III had ordered or was complicitous in their deaths.
1 CONT
1 CONT Horace Walpole, Josephine Tey and Valerie Anand are among writers who have argued strongly that King Richard was innocent of the death of the Princes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard III appears in the 2002 List of "100 Great Britons" (sponsored by the BBC and voted for by the public), alongside such others as David Beckham and Johnny Rotten. The BBC History Magazine lists him under "doubtful entrants,
1 CONC  based on special interest lobbying or 'cult' status", and comments: "On the list owing to the Ricardian lobby, but a minor monarch".
1 CONT
1 CONT In spite of having died aged only 32, he is often depicted as being considerably older. Basil Rathbone and Peter Cook were both 46 when they played him, Laurence Olivier was 48, Vincent Price was 51, and Ian McKellen was 56.
1 CONT
