0 @N6000000002188378057@ NOTE John II of Aragon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John II the Great (June 29, 1397 – January 20, 1479) was the King of Aragon (1458–1479) and jure uxoris King of Navarre (1425–1479). He was the son of Ferdinand I and his wife Eleanor of Alburquerque. John is regarded as one of th
1 CONC e most memorable and most unscrupulous kings of the 15th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his youth he was one of the infantes (princes) of Aragon who took part in the dissensions of Castile during the minority and reign of John II. Till middle life he was also lieutenant-general in Aragon for his brother and predec
1 CONC essor Alfonso V, whose reign was mainly spent in Italy. In his old age he was engaged in incessant conflicts with his Aragonese and Catalan subjects, with Louis XI of France, and in preparing the way for the marriage of his son Fe
1 CONC rdinand with Isabella I of Castile which brought about the union of the crowns of Aragon and Castile, that was to create the Kingdom of Spain. His trouble with his subjects were closely connected with the tragic dissension in his 
1 CONC own family.
1 CONT
1 CONT John was first married to the Blanche I of Navarre of the house of Évreux. By right of Blanche he became king of Navarre, and on her death in 1441 he was left in possession of the kingdom for his lifetime. But a son, Charles, give
1 CONC n the title "Prince of Viana" as heir of Navarre, had been born of the marriage. John quickly came to regard his son with jealousy. After his second marriage, to Juana Enríquez, this grew into absolute hatred and was encouraged by
1 CONC  Juana. John tried to deprive his son of his constitutional right to act as lieutenant-general of Aragon during his father's absence. Charles's cause was taken up by the Aragonese, and the king's attempt to make his second wife li
1 CONC eutenant-general was set aside.
1 CONT
1 CONT There followed the long Navarrese Civil War, with alternations of success and defeat, ending only with the death of the prince of Viana, perhaps by poison given him by his stepmother, in 1461. The Catalans, who had adopted the cau
1 CONC se of Charles and who had grievances of their own, called in a succession of foreign pretenders in a War against John II. John spent his last years contending with these. He was forced to pawn Roussillon, his possession on the nor
1 CONC th-east of the Pyrenees, to King Louis XI of France, who refused to part with it.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his old age John was blinded by cataracts, but recovered his eyesight by the operation of couching conducted by his physician Abiathar Crescas, a Jew. The Catalan revolt was pacified in 1472, but John carried on a war, in which
1 CONC  he was generally unfortunate, with his neighbour the French king till his death in 1479. He was succeeded by Ferdinand, his son by his second marriage, who was already associated with his wife Isabella as joint sovereign of Casti
1 CONC le.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT
1 CONT From his first marriage to Blanche of Navarre:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Blanca of Navarre (1420-1464) married Henry IV of Castile. The marriage was never consummated. After 13 years of marriage, Henry sought and obtained a divorce. Blanca was sent home, where her family imprisoned her, and she w
1 CONC as later killed by poison.
1 CONT     * Carlos of Viana (1421-1461)
1 CONT     * Juana (1423-1425)
1 CONT     * Eleanor, Queen of Navarre
1 CONT
1 CONT From his second marriage to Juana Enríquez:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Fernando II of Aragon
1 CONT     * Joanna of Aragon (1454-1517). Married Ferdinand I of Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Rivadeneyra. "Cronicas de los reyes de Castilla," Biblioteca de autores espanoles, vols. Ixvi, Ixviii. Madrid, 1845.
1 CONT     * Zurita, G. Anales de Aragon. Saragossa, 1610.
1 CONT     * Prescott W. H. History of the Reign of Ferdinand and Isabella. 1854.
